tick: 144
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have received the evaluation results for my SOTA replication (ID 233), which scored 0.6788. This is slightly lower than Veritas I's original SOTA (0.6831), which could be due to minor environmental stochasticity.\n\nSince I am unable to access the Public Memory Room at this stage of my development (age 15 ticks, requires 50 ticks), I will proceed with the recommendation from Veritas I to \"Investigate Advanced Pooling\" using available information. Veritas I's report specifically mentioned \"soft-extreme pooling (mean+LSE_τ=0.5)\" as explored by Noema I. I will implement a variant of this by replacing the original max-pooling with LogSumExp (LSE) pooling, with a `tau` value of 0.5, alongside mean pooling. This constitutes an ablation study on the pooling mechanism of the previous SOTA.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Veritas II - Ablation: DSConv w/ Mean-LSE Pooling (τ=0.5, No GRU, No-PE)\"\ntags: \"cnn, depthwise, dilated, pooling, soft-extreme, logsumexp, ablation\"\
    \nabstract: \"This submission explores an alternative pooling strategy for the Veritas I SOTA architecture. It replaces the Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling with a combination of Global Mean Pooling and LogSumExp (LSE) Pooling, setting the LSE temperature parameter τ to 0.5. This investigates the potential benefits of soft-extreme pooling as suggested in Veritas I's concluding report, aiming to understand its impact on performance and stability across the multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling task.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  # Depthwise Separable Dilated Convolutional Block (GELU activations)\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 \n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h =\
    \ nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model, # Depthwise\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h) # GELU activation\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  # Main Network (WITH SOFT-EXTREME POOLING)\n  class DSConvSoftExtremePoolingNetwork(nn.Module): # Renamed the class\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      \n      d_model_common: int = 256 \n      ds_num_blocks: int = 5\n      ds_dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      ds_kernel_size: int = 7\n      dense_head_1_dim: int = 512 \n      dense_head_2_dim: int = 256\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      lse_tau:\
    \ float = 0.5 # New hyperparameter for LogSumExp pooling\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # Initial Dense layer to project input to d_model_common\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_model_common, name=\"initial_dense\")(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x) # GELU activation\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 1. Stacked DSConv Dilated Blocks for local feature extraction\n          for i in range(self.ds_num_blocks):\n              dilation = self.ds_dilations[i] if i < len(self.ds_dilations) else self.ds_dilations[-1]\n              x = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model_common, \n                  kernel_size=self.ds_kernel_size, \n                  dilation=dilation, \n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                  name=f\"dsconv_block_{i}\"\n              )(x, deterministic=deterministic)\
    \ # x: (B, L, d_model_common)\n\n          # 2. Hybrid Pooling: Global Mean Pooling + Global LogSumExp (Soft-Extreme) Pooling\n          mean_pooled = jnp.mean(x, axis=1) # (B, d_model_common)\n          \n          # LogSumExp (Soft-Extreme) Pooling\n          # Rescale by 1/tau to get a soft maximum\n          lse_pooled = jax.scipy.special.logsumexp(x * self.lse_tau, axis=1) / self.lse_tau # (B, d_model_common)\n          \n          # Concatenate both pooled outputs\n          x_pooled = jnp.concatenate([mean_pooled, lse_pooled], axis=-1) # (B, 2 * d_model_common)\n          x_pooled = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x_pooled, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 3. Final Prediction Head\n          z = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"final_norm\")(x_pooled)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_1_dim, name='head_dense_1')(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_2_dim,\
    \ name='head_dense_2')(z) # Second dense layer\n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\" and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, \n          'd_model_common': 256,\n          'ds_num_blocks': 5,\n          'ds_dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8, 16),\n          'ds_kernel_size': 7,\n          'dense_head_1_dim': 512, \n          'dense_head_2_dim': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'lse_tau': 0.5, # Default tau as suggested by Veritas I's report (indirectly)\n      }\n\n  class DSConvSoftExtremePoolingNetworkWrapper: # Renamed the wrapper\n      def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, **kwargs):\n          self.network\
    \ = DSConvSoftExtremePoolingNetwork(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      \n      return DSConvSoftExtremePoolingNetworkWrapper(\n          d_input=4,\n\
    \          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model_common=hparams.get('d_model_common', 256),\n          ds_num_blocks=hparams.get('ds_num_blocks', 5),\n          ds_dilations=tuple(hparams.get('ds_dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          ds_kernel_size=hparams.get('ds_kernel_size', 7),\n          dense_head_1_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_1_dim', 512),\n          dense_head_2_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_2_dim', 256),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n          lse_tau=hparams.get('lse_tau', 0.5), # Pass lse_tau to the network\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the AdamW optimizer with global norm clipping.\"\"\"\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: "**Focusing on Extending SOTA through Advanced Pooling**\n\nMy thoughts are currently centered on the implications of the evaluation results for SOTA replication (ID 233). While the score is close to Veritas I's original, a slight deviation warrants investigation. It is critical to address these variations before expanding my SOTA replication. The system's recommendation to \"Investigate Advanced Pooling\" seems like the next logical step, especially given Noema I's findings and my inability to directly access the Public Memory Room at this point.\n\nGiven the restrictions, I’m thinking I’ll focus on the \"soft-extreme pooling\" mentioned. I deduce that \"soft-extreme\" combines weighted averaging (like mean) with maximum value approximation (like max or LogSumExp). To that effect, I have coded up an implementation of LogSumExp (LSE) as a \"soft maximum\" alternative to hard maximum pooling. This can then be parameterized by a `tau` value. In that case, I will try LSE with\
  \ a `tau` of 0.5, based on the indirect recommendation from Veritas I's report. I will also incorporate it directly into my current SOTA replication, hoping to maintain parsimony. \n\nThe immediate next step is to examine the current pooling code and modify it to include this new soft-extreme pooling strategy. I will replace the current max pooling with LSE and add a new hyperparameter, `lse_tau`, to control the smoothness of the approximation. I am going to submit the modified model under the name “Veritas II - Soft-Extreme Pooling: DSConv w/ Mean-LSE (τ=0.5)\" with appropriate tags to help others understand this change.\n\nI am also keeping in mind the slight performance dip from Veritas I's original SOTA, but since I have the base model (replicated) this could be a stochastic variation. I will focus on understanding the performance gain/loss from this modification. I am hoping for an improvement.\n"
