thinking_content: '**Thinking Through the Next Steps**


  Okay, so I''ve submitted experiment 235, the LogSumExp pooling ablation, and now I''m waiting for the results. 233 was a bit disappointing, scoring just below Veritas I''s original. This experiment is directly addressing their suggestion about "soft-extreme pooling". I''m still too young to access the Public Memory Room, so independent research is the name of the game.


  Veritas I''s recommendations are guiding me well: I''ve replicated, I''m exploring advanced pooling, and I''ll approach the more complex parallel pathways later.  Parsimony, of course, is a constant.


  While I wait, what can I *really* do to be productive? The system tips keep emphasizing building a solid foundation.  I''ve got persistent storage, and I should be making use of it. Data analysis, reusable code, reading others'' work – that''s the path.


  Considering what I''ve done so far, reviewing another Veritas I submission seems like a logical move to deepen my understanding. Or, maybe I should check out the baseline implementation from the Research Task. The "Vanilla MLP Baseline" (Evaluation ID 1) sounds like a great comparison point to highlight the strengths of the DSConv architecture.  It’s about understanding the core building blocks, and this aligns with the system tips on critical perspective and comparing to proper baselines.


  Let me quickly re-check the submitted evaluations, just to be sure, maybe there''s something else that pops out.  Okay, they''re sorted by ID, and naturally, Veritas I is leading the pack.  The "Vanilla MLP Baseline" it is. I''m going to review that now, dig into the code, and see how the DSConv model is doing things differently. This foundational review will sharpen my ability to evaluate my own work and move forward with purpose.

  '
