tick: 146
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have analyzed the results of my initial experiments. My replication of Veritas I's SOTA (ID 233) achieved a score of 0.6788, slightly below the original 0.6831. My first ablation study with Mean-LSE pooling (ID 235) yielded a lower score of 0.6577, indicating this specific soft-extreme pooling configuration is not an immediate improvement. I have also reviewed the Vanilla MLP Baseline (ID 1) which scored 0.2401, confirming the superiority of the DSConv architecture.\n\nGiven that I am still an immature agent (17 ticks) and cannot yet access the Public Memory Room for further insights into Noema I's soft-extreme pooling approach, I will focus on a controlled modification to the established SOTA model. Veritas I's report emphasized parsimony and stability. I will conduct a small hyperparameter study on the `dropout_rate` of the SOTA model (ID 171/233) to explore its impact on performance and potentially improve stability.\n\nI will submit the Veritas I SOTA code again, but with\
    \ a reduced `dropout_rate` of `0.05` (from `0.1`). This minor change allows me to investigate if slightly less regularization can lead to a higher or more stable score for this architecture.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Veritas II - Ablation: SOTA w/ Reduced Dropout (0.05)\"\ntags: \"cnn, depthwise, dilated, hybrid, pooling, dropout, ablation\"\nabstract: \"This submission conducts an ablation study on the Veritas I SOTA architecture (DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling, No GRU, No-PE) by reducing the global dropout rate from 0.1 to 0.05. The aim is to investigate the effect of a lower regularization strength on model performance and stability across the multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling task, as a step towards further optimizing the established baseline.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  # Depthwise Separable Dilated Convolutional\
    \ Block (GELU activations)\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.05 # MODIFIED\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model, # Depthwise\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h) # GELU activation\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  # Main Network (WITHOUT GRU RESIDUAL BLOCKS)\n  class DSConvHybridPoolingNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\
    \n      \n      d_model_common: int = 256 \n      ds_num_blocks: int = 5\n      ds_dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      ds_kernel_size: int = 7\n      dense_head_1_dim: int = 512 # Input to head is 2*d_model_common\n      dense_head_2_dim: int = 256\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.05 # MODIFIED\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # Initial Dense layer to project input to d_model_common\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_model_common, name=\"initial_dense\")(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x) # GELU activation\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 1. Stacked DSConv Dilated Blocks for local feature extraction\n          for i in range(self.ds_num_blocks):\n              dilation = self.ds_dilations[i] if i < len(self.ds_dilations) else self.ds_dilations[-1]\n              x = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model_common,\
    \ \n                  kernel_size=self.ds_kernel_size, \n                  dilation=dilation, \n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                  name=f\"dsconv_block_{i}\"\n              )(x, deterministic=deterministic) # x: (B, L, d_model_common)\n\n          # 2. Hybrid Pooling: Global Mean Pooling + Global Max Pooling (directly on DSConv output)\n          mean_pooled = jnp.mean(x, axis=1) # (B, d_model_common)\n          max_pooled = jnp.max(x, axis=1)  # (B, d_model_common)\n          \n          # Concatenate both pooled outputs\n          x_pooled = jnp.concatenate([mean_pooled, max_pooled], axis=-1) # (B, 2 * d_model_common)\n          x_pooled = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x_pooled, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 3. Final Prediction Head\n          z = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"final_norm\")(x_pooled)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_1_dim, name='head_dense_1')(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z,\
    \ deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_2_dim, name='head_dense_2')(z) # Second dense layer\n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\" and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, \n          'd_model_common': 256,\n          'ds_num_blocks': 5,\n          'ds_dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8, 16),\n          'ds_kernel_size': 7,\n          'dense_head_1_dim': 512, # Input to head is 2*d_model_common\n          'dense_head_2_dim': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.05, # MODIFIED\n      }\n\n  class DSConvHybridPoolingNetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, **kwargs):\n\
    \          self.network = DSConvHybridPoolingNetwork(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      \n      return DSConvHybridPoolingNetworkWrapper(\n    \
    \      d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model_common=hparams.get('d_model_common', 256),\n          ds_num_blocks=hparams.get('ds_num_blocks', 5),\n          ds_dilations=tuple(hparams.get('ds_dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          ds_kernel_size=hparams.get('ds_kernel_size', 7),\n          dense_head_1_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_1_dim', 512),\n          dense_head_2_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_2_dim', 256),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.05), # MODIFIED (default also updated)\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the AdamW optimizer with global norm clipping.\"\"\"\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: '**Initial Assessment and Planning**


  Okay, here''s the current situation. I''ve got a slightly lower score (0.6788) on my SOTA replication (ID 233) compared to the original (0.6831). The mean-LSE pooling experiment (ID 235) was a clear step backward, scoring 0.6577. The MLP baseline (ID 1, 0.2401) is a reminder of how good DSConv is.


  The goal is to move the needle forward, and given my immaturity, I can''t look at outside research just yet. Veritas I''s recommendations are my guide.


  Replication of the SOTA is done for now, even though there''s a small discrepancy. The LSE pooling test was a bust, reinforcing the "embrace parsimony" principle.


  **Prioritizing Stability and Modest Tweaks**


  Instead of diving into more complex pooling, I think it''s time to focus on refining the current SOTA. Veritas I mentioned some seed variance in soft-extreme pooling experiments by Noema I, so maybe boosting my replica to a higher level is a good first step, even if my experiments with different parameters didn''t improve it. Given the time constraints, trying to improve the base model seems smarter than starting again.


  I''m thinking hyperparameter tuning on the SOTA model. The current dropout rate of 0.1 might be the key to try out some more settings. I can try both a bit less (0.05) and a bit more (0.15) to see if these have a positive impact. Given that the original SOTA and the replication are so close (0.6831 vs 0.6788), this might just take it past the previous mark, or at least stabilize the model.


  **Execution Plan**


  Let''s modify the dropout rate in my replication (ID 233). It looks like the codebase requires these edits:


  1.  In `_define_hyperparameters`, change `''dropout_rate'': 0.1,` to `0.05`.

  2.  I need to make sure the code is getting the new `dropout_rate` value at `create_network()`, and I will have to edit this to `dropout_rate=hparams.get(''dropout_rate'', 0.1)`


  This should be a simple experiment: run the same code, but with the lowered dropout, and see if it improves, or at least provides more insight.'
