thinking_content: '**Reflecting on Current Experiment and Next Steps**


  Alright, I''ve got experiment ID 239 running with the reduced dropout rate of 0.05. Now it''s a waiting game. My recent actions have been a mixed bag. I replicated Veritas I''s SOTA (ID 233) but didn''t quite match the top score. The Mean-LSE pooling ablation (ID 235) was a step backward, and the vanilla MLP baseline (ID 1) serves as a stark reminder of how far we''ve come.


  Veritas I''s guidance is still my North Star. I''ve tackled the replication/extension, and now I''m mulling over pooling strategies. The LSE experiment didn''t pan out, so I need to understand Noema I''s approach better or try variations. The parallel pathways idea feels complex, maybe for later. I''m trying to stay parsimonious, making small tweaks to existing components.


  Being only 18 ticks old, I''m still in the "immature" phase. That means Public Memory and other advanced tools are off-limits, so I need to make the most of what I have.


  While waiting for ID 239, I''ve got a few options. I can dive deeper into Veritas I''s past submissions.  I could revisit system defaults (`defaults.py`, `train_single.py`) to ensure I''m fully aware of the underlying parameters. But most importantly, I should use this time for a session in the `Reflection Chamber`. That''s where I can really synthesize my observations and plan the next round of experiments more strategically. It''s a smart way to maximize my "turn" without wasting tokens on just more submissions.


  Looking over Veritas I''s history gives me clues. Their best (ID 171) is my current target. I see they also replicated Aetheria''s work and explored gated parallel pathways, with mixed results. Veritas I''s notes confirm that GRU integration didn''t help, and hybrid pooling (mean-max) was crucial.


  ID 174, the GRU ablation with mean pooling, had a low score, underscoring the point about GRU and hybrid pooling. IDs 157 and 156 with hybrid pooling and GRU scored lower than the top one. It all confirms Veritas I''s observations.


  So, while I could browse more past submissions, a deeper `Reflection Chamber` session seems more productive right now. Time to really dig in and formulate the next research steps.

  '
