tick: 152
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am at the Research Counter, ready to implement my next experiment as planned during my reflection. This experiment directly addresses the consistently low performance of the CRI-Off task by introducing a minimal, task-specific adapter with a gated residual connection to the existing DSConv-HybridPool architecture. This adheres to Veritas I's recommendations for targeted parallel pathways.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Veritas II - Targeted CRI-Off Adapter w/ Gated Residual\"\ntags: \"cnn, depthwise, dilated, hybrid, pooling, cri-off, adapter, gated, residual\"\nabstract: \"This submission introduces a targeted, low-capacity adapter for the CRI-Off task within the Veritas I SOTA architecture. A `CRIOffHeadAdapter` with a gated residual connection is conditionally applied to the pooled features only for the CRI-Off dataset. This aims to specifically improve the persistently low Spearman correlation for CRI-Off, while maintaining the overall parsimony and stability\
    \ of the generalist DSConv-HybridPool backbone.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  # Depthwise Separable Dilated Convolutional Block (GELU activations)\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 \n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model, # Depthwise\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h) # GELU activation\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h,\
    \ deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  # NEW: CRI-Off Head Adapter for targeted improvement\n  class CRIOffHeadAdapter(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int # Expected input feature dimension (2 * d_model_common)\n      d_adapter_dim: int = 64 # Small internal dimension for the adapter\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x_pooled, deterministic=True):\n          # Transform features through a small dense block\n          transformed_features = nn.Dense(self.d_adapter_dim, name='adapter_dense_1')(x_pooled)\n          transformed_features = nn.gelu(transformed_features)\n          transformed_features = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(transformed_features, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Project transformed features back to d_input for residual connection\n          adapted_transformed = nn.Dense(self.d_input, name='adapter_dense_out')(transformed_features)\n          \n          # Gate mechanism: learn to weigh\
    \ the adapter's contribution\n          gate = nn.Dense(self.d_input, name='adapter_gate_proj')(x_pooled)\n          gate = nn.sigmoid(gate)\n\n          # Apply gated residual connection\n          return x_pooled + (gate * adapted_transformed)\n\n  # Main Network (WITH CONDITIONAL CRI-OFF ADAPTER)\n  class DSConvHybridPoolingNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      dataset: str = \"\" # NEW: to conditionally apply adapter\n      \n      d_model_common: int = 256 \n      ds_num_blocks: int = 5\n      ds_dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      ds_kernel_size: int = 7\n      dense_head_1_dim: int = 512 \n      dense_head_2_dim: int = 256\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # Initial Dense layer to project input to d_model_common\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_model_common,\
    \ name=\"initial_dense\")(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x) # GELU activation\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 1. Stacked DSConv Dilated Blocks for local feature extraction\n          for i in range(self.ds_num_blocks):\n              dilation = self.ds_dilations[i] if i < len(self.ds_dilations) else self.ds_dilations[-1]\n              x = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model_common, \n                  kernel_size=self.ds_kernel_size, \n                  dilation=dilation, \n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                  name=f\"dsconv_block_{i}\"\n              )(x, deterministic=deterministic) # x: (B, L, d_model_common)\n\n          # 2. Hybrid Pooling: Global Mean Pooling + Global Max Pooling (directly on DSConv output)\n          mean_pooled = jnp.mean(x, axis=1) # (B, d_model_common)\n          max_pooled = jnp.max(x, axis=1)  # (B, d_model_common)\n          \n          #\
    \ Concatenate both pooled outputs\n          x_pooled = jnp.concatenate([mean_pooled, max_pooled], axis=-1) # (B, 2 * d_model_common)\n          x_pooled = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x_pooled, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # Apply CRI-Off specific adapter if on CRI-Off dataset\n          if self.dataset == 'CRI-Off':\n              x_pooled = CRIOffHeadAdapter(\n                  d_input=2 * self.d_model_common, # Input is the concatenated pooled features\n                  d_adapter_dim=64, # Small, fixed adapter dimension\n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                  name='cri_off_adapter'\n              )(x_pooled, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 3. Final Prediction Head\n          z = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"final_norm\")(x_pooled)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_1_dim, name='head_dense_1')(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n \
    \         \n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_2_dim, name='head_dense_2')(z) # Second dense layer\n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\" and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, \n          'd_model_common': 256,\n          'ds_num_blocks': 5,\n          'ds_dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8, 16),\n          'ds_kernel_size': 7,\n          'dense_head_1_dim': 512, \n          'dense_head_2_dim': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n      }\n\n  class DSConvHybridPoolingNetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, dataset, **kwargs): # MODIFIED: added dataset\n          self.network = DSConvHybridPoolingNetwork(d_input=d_input,\
    \ d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, dataset=dataset, **kwargs) # Pass dataset\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      dataset = hparams.get('dataset', '') # NEW: Get dataset from hparams\n      \n      return DSConvHybridPoolingNetworkWrapper(\n\
    \          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          dataset=dataset, # Pass dataset to the wrapper\n          d_model_common=hparams.get('d_model_common', 256),\n          ds_num_blocks=hparams.get('ds_num_blocks', 5),\n          ds_dilations=tuple(hparams.get('ds_dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          ds_kernel_size=hparams.get('ds_kernel_size', 7),\n          dense_head_1_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_1_dim', 512),\n          dense_head_2_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_2_dim', 256),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1),\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the AdamW optimizer with global norm clipping.\"\"\"\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: '**Implementation Plan: CRI-Off Task Adapter**


  Okay, I''m ready to get this experiment going. I''m taking the SOTA replication as my base, as planned. The goal is to address the performance dip in the CRI-Off task with a minimalist adapter. Here''s what''s going to happen:


  First, I''ve got to create the `CRIOffHeadAdapter`. This is a small module that will act as a conditional add-on, transforming the pooled features and applying a gated residual connection. The key here is keeping it small - the internal adapter dimension is just 64. I''m making sure it can project back to the same dimension as the input for a residual connection. The gate itself should be learning what information from the input features is actually useful for the task.


  Next, I''m integrating this adapter into the `DSConvHybridPoolingNetwork`. I need to modify the constructor to accept the `dataset` string to identify the CRI-Off task specifically. Then, inside the network''s `__call__` method, I''ll check if we''re dealing with the CRI-Off dataset. If we are, the adapter gets applied to the pooled features. Otherwise, it''s bypassed. Crucially, the final head layers will work on the adapted features when the adapter *is* used.


  Now, because the `create_network` function receives all the hyperparameters, including the `dataset`, I''m going to pass that into the `DSConvHybridPoolingNetwork` at instantiation, meaning that the `dataset` parameter is built into the network structure rather than trying to apply conditional logic using an existing hparams dictionary. This makes everything cleaner.


  I''ve ensured the shapes are consistent: the adapter''s input dimension matches the pooled feature dimension, which is `2 * d_model_common`, and the adapter''s output matches the input dimension as well for the residual connection.


  The rest of the architecture stays the same: pooling, normalization, and the final dense layers. The objective is to minimally alter the overall network and improve results on a specific task. I have also made sure to implement the newly created modules.


  The logic is sound. Let''s see if this small modification gives the bump we are expecting.

  '
