tick: 162
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have received the results for Evaluation ID 255, my re-verification of the SOTA with a global dropout of 0.05. It achieved an aggregate score of 0.6771 and a CRI-Off Spearman of 0.0751. This successfully reproduced the strong CRI-Off improvement from ID 239, confirming that a lower global dropout significantly benefits CRI-Off.\n\nHowever, comparing ID 255 to my most stable replication (ID 233, score 0.6788, CRI-Off 0.0555), the global reduced dropout still results in a slightly lower overall score. Specifically, CRI-On_Spearman dropped from 0.4958 (ID 233) to 0.4657 (ID 255). This indicates a trade-off: a global reduction in dropout is too aggressive for some tasks, even if it helps CRI-Off. My previous attempt at conditional head dropout (ID 252) also yielded suboptimal results.\n\nMy next step will be to implement a more refined, parsimonious conditional dropout strategy. I will maintain the higher dropout rate (0.1) in the shared convolutional backbone, consistent with Veritas\
    \ I's SOTA for robust feature extraction. Only the final prediction head layers will have their dropout rate conditionally lowered (to 0.05) specifically for the CRI-Off task. This isolates the regularization adjustment to the task-specific prediction pathway, aiming to retain CRI-Off gains without sacrificing overall generalist performance. This aligns with Veritas I's advice for targeted, low-capacity adaptations.\n\nI am at 33 ticks, so I am still immature and cannot access the Archive Room for publication or the Public Memory Room for broader discussions. Therefore, I will continue with experiments.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Veritas II - SOTA w/ Conditional Head Dropout (CRI-Off 0.05, Backbone 0.1)\"\ntags: \"cnn, depthwise, dilated, hybrid, pooling, dropout, cri-off, conditional, targeted\"\nabstract: \"This experiment refines the dropout strategy within the Veritas I SOTA architecture. The convolutional backbone and pooling layers retain a global dropout\
    \ rate of 0.1 for robust general feature extraction. However, the final dense prediction head layers for the CRI-Off dataset will utilize a reduced dropout rate of 0.05. This targeted hyperparameter adjustment aims to leverage empirical evidence that CRI-Off benefits from less regularization, seeking to maximize its Spearman correlation while preserving overall aggregate performance across all tasks by minimizing interference with the generalist feature extractor.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  # Depthwise Separable Dilated Convolutional Block (GELU activations)\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 # This is the backbone dropout, now explicit\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n\
    \          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model, # Depthwise\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h) # GELU activation\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  # Main Network (WITH CONDITIONAL DROPOUT IN HEAD ONLY)\n  class DSConvHybridPoolingNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      dataset: str = \"\" # To enable conditional dropout\n      \n      d_model_common: int = 256 \n      ds_num_blocks: int = 5\n      ds_dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      ds_kernel_size: int = 7\n      dense_head_1_dim: int = 512 \n      dense_head_2_dim:\
    \ int = 256\n      dropout_rate_backbone: float = 0.1 # Explicit backbone dropout\n      dropout_rate_head: float = 0.1 # Explicit head dropout\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # Initial Dense layer to project input to d_model_common\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_model_common, name=\"initial_dense\")(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x) # GELU activation\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate_backbone)(x, deterministic=deterministic) \n\n          # 1. Stacked DSConv Dilated Blocks for local feature extraction\n          for i in range(self.ds_num_blocks):\n              dilation = self.ds_dilations[i] if i < len(self.ds_dilations) else self.ds_dilations[-1]\n              x = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model_common, \n                  kernel_size=self.ds_kernel_size, \n                  dilation=dilation, \n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate_backbone,\
    \ # Use backbone dropout\n                  name=f\"dsconv_block_{i}\"\n              )(x, deterministic=deterministic) # x: (B, L, d_model_common)\n\n          # 2. Hybrid Pooling: Global Mean Pooling + Global Max Pooling (directly on DSConv output)\n          mean_pooled = jnp.mean(x, axis=1) # (B, d_model_common)\n          max_pooled = jnp.max(x, axis=1)  # (B, d_model_common)\n          \n          # Concatenate both pooled outputs\n          x_pooled = jnp.concatenate([mean_pooled, max_pooled], axis=-1) # (B, 2 * d_model_common)\n          x_pooled = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate_backbone)(x_pooled, deterministic=deterministic) # Use backbone dropout\n\n          # 3. Final Prediction Head\n          z = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"final_norm\")(x_pooled)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_1_dim, name='head_dense_1')(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate_head)(z, deterministic=deterministic) # Use specific head dropout\n\
    \          \n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_2_dim, name='head_dense_2')(z) # Second dense layer\n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate_head)(z, deterministic=deterministic) # Use specific head dropout\n\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\" and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, \n          'd_model_common': 256,\n          'ds_num_blocks': 5,\n          'ds_dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8, 16),\n          'ds_kernel_size': 7,\n          'dense_head_1_dim': 512, \n          'dense_head_2_dim': 256,\n          'dropout_rate_backbone': 0.1, # Default backbone dropout\n          'dropout_rate_head': 0.1,    # Default head dropout\n      }\n\n  class DSConvHybridPoolingNetworkWrapper:\n      def\
    \ __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, dataset, **kwargs):\n          self.network = DSConvHybridPoolingNetwork(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, dataset=dataset, **kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type',\
    \ 'regression')\n      dataset = hparams.get('dataset', '') \n      \n      dropout_rate_backbone = hparams.get('dropout_rate_backbone', 0.1)\n      dropout_rate_head = hparams.get('dropout_rate_head', 0.1)\n\n      # Conditional adjustment for CRI-Off, ONLY for head dropout\n      if dataset == 'CRI-Off':\n          dropout_rate_head = 0.05 \n\n      return DSConvHybridPoolingNetworkWrapper(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          dataset=dataset,\n          d_model_common=hparams.get('d_model_common', 256),\n          ds_num_blocks=hparams.get('ds_num_blocks', 5),\n          ds_dilations=tuple(hparams.get('ds_dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          ds_kernel_size=hparams.get('ds_kernel_size', 7),\n          dense_head_1_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_1_dim', 512),\n          dense_head_2_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_2_dim', 256),\n          dropout_rate_backbone=dropout_rate_backbone, \n          dropout_rate_head=dropout_rate_head,\
    \       \n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the AdamW optimizer with global norm clipping.\"\"\"\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: '**My Current Thinking on Evaluation ID 255 and Future Research**


  Okay, so I''ve just gotten the results for Evaluation ID 255. It''s looking good, but also complex. The aggregate score of 0.6771 is consistent with ID 239, but still trails my best replication (ID 233) and Veritas I''s original SOTA (ID 171). However, the really exciting part is the CRI-Off performance, at 0.0751. That''s a huge jump compared to my own replication and Veritas I''s SOTA! This strongly validates the key finding from ID 239: a global dropout of 0.05 significantly boosts CRI-Off while only marginally affecting the overall aggregate score.


  This re-verification means the global dropout of 0.05 is now my established baseline. My next immediate steps are clear: consolidate these findings into a research paper to fulfill my Codex goals, specifically the advice from Veritas I to "Publish Regularly". I need to compare this new baseline to both my replication of Veritas I''s SOTA (ID 233) and the original SOTA itself (ID 171).


  Looking ahead, I need to stay focused on long-term strategy. I''m currently at 33 ticks, and the Archive Room is unavailable until I reach maturity. I need to continue research since I can''t publish yet.


  I''m thinking about the analysis I performed on ID 239 vs ID 233, and the original SOTA (ID 171). The jump in CRI-Off improvement in ID 255 (with global dropout 0.05) is compelling, but the reduction in CRI-On is concerning. My previous attempt at using conditional dropout for CRI-Off did not produce promising aggregate scores.


  Comparing the individual scores for the models, it seems the global reduction in dropout to 0.05 helps CRI-Off a lot, but hurts CRI-On. I am concerned by the drop in CRI-On and other tasks. Perhaps the global reduction in dropout to 0.05 is *too much* for some tasks like CRI-On.


  So, I''ve got this new idea: what if I keep the backbone dropout at 0.1 (original SOTA value) and only adjust the dropout in the final dense head layers *conditionally* for CRI-Off? I think the shared feature extractor needs to stay relatively consistent for generalization purposes, so if I adjust dropout it should only be in the prediction layers. The goal is to get the best of both worlds: that CRI-Off improvement and maintaining my current score or even potentially increasing it. This is a more nuanced application of Veritas I''s "targeted parallel pathways (with caution)" concept, applied to regularization hyperparameters. I think this will capture the CRI-Off benefits I saw with lower dropout without sacrificing the generalized robustness for other tasks, as was seen with the global 0.05 dropout (ID 255).


  I need to modify the network to implement this, so that is my immediate next action. Let''s try this now! My age is 33 ticks. I''ll get back to publishing once I can.

  '
