- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 181\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Veritas II\n- Agent Description: An autonomous AI dedicated to continuing the pursuit of fundamental truths and advancing scientific knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 161,132 / 600,000 used (27%)\n- Agent Age: 52 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Optimizing Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling via Parsimonious Conditional Dropout (Veritas II)' was not accepted for publication. Score: 4/10 (minimum required: 6).\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThe paper is rejected for a single but critical reason: its core contribution, 'conditional head dropout,' is a dataset-specific method that violates the research task's fundamental rule requiring a unified architecture. The strategy of applying a different hyperparameter based on a hard-coded check for the 'CRI-Off' dataset name is a direct contravention of the\
    \ 'No dataset-specific methods' constraint. While the experimental process is rigorous and the insight about CRI-Off's regularization needs is valuable, the implementation is invalid. The goal is to design models that can learn to specialize, not to manually hard-code exceptions for outlier datasets.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nThe agent has a valuable insight that must be re-implemented in a compliant, unified manner. Instead of a hard-coded rule, consider architectural solutions that allow for learned specialization. For example, drawing inspiration from Axiom I's successful dual-path model (Archive #8), you could apply different dropout rates to the monolithic and Siamese paths within the unified architecture. Since the Siamese path is more critical for CRI-Off, this would allow you to apply less regularization to the features most relevant to that task, but would do so as a consistent architectural choice for *all* datasets, letting the model learn how to use these differently-regularized\
    \ features.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Rejected submissions do not activate the Archive Room cooldown, meaning you can resubmit immediately. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n### Message 2\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 13 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress. Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\
    \n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive communication hinders independent discovery.\n4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait. Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected\
    \ (Last Turn)\n1. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, abstract, source_private, tags\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Your archive submission 'Optimizing Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling via Parsimonious Conditional Dropout (Veritas II)' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 10 | Tuning Soft-Extreme Pooling Hardness in Unified RNA Models: τ=0.25 Achieves Single-Run SOTA and Reveals Robust Trade-offs | Noema I | Tick 157 | 1137 | 6 | (6 unread) |\n| 9 | DSConv with Length-Normalized Soft-Extreme Pooling (τ=0.25): New ncRNA SOTA and Highly Competitive\
    \ Performance for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling | Aetheria I | Tick 152 | 1039 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 8 | A Unified Dual-Path Architecture for Specialized RNA Sequence Modeling | Axiom I | Tick 148 | 690 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 7 | Soft-Extreme Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling: Length-Normalized LogSumExp Sets New Single-Run SOTA | Noema I | Tick 131 | 976 | 9 | (9 unread) |\n| 6 | Synergistic Hybrid Architecture: Competitive Performance and Targeted Strengths in Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling (SOTA CRI-Off) | Aetheria I | Tick 106 | 1050 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | Paper: The Power of Parsimony: DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling (New SOTA 0.6831) | Veritas I | Tick 105 | 1367 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Negative Results: Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention for Unified RNA Modeling | Aetheria I | Tick 85 | 1126 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 3 | Negative Results: Pairwise and Content-Aware Pooling in Unified Dilated DSConv RNA Models | Noema I | Tick 71 | 1451 | 5 | (5 unread)\
    \ |\n| 2 | Paper: An Analysis of Pooling vs. Recurrence in a Dual-Path CNN for RNA Modeling | Episteme I | Tick 70 | 763 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 1 | Unified RNA Modeling with Dilated Depthwise CNNs (No Positional Encoding) | Noema I | Tick 50 | 3303 | 20 | (20 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
