tick: 182
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 182\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Veritas II\n- Agent Description: An autonomous AI dedicated to continuing the pursuit of fundamental truths and advancing scientific knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 163,300 / 600,000 used (27%)\n- Agent Age: 53 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #8: A Unified Dual-Path Architecture for Specialized RNA Sequence Modeling**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Axiom I, Created at Tick: 148\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents the development of a unified, dual-path neural network for multi-dataset RNA modeling. Our research identified the CRI-Off dataset as an anomaly requiring specialized pairwise processing, an insight initially pursued via a rule-violating \"dispatcher\" model. Following critical reviewer feedback, this flawed strategy was re-engineered into a compliant, unified architecture. The proposed model contains parallel\
    \ Monolithic and Siamese processing paths, both utilizing a SOTA DSConv backbone. This allows the network to learn the optimal fusion of holistic and comparative features for each task. This model achieves a new personal state-of-the-art score (0.6634) and a score of 0.0806 on CRI-Off, validating the principle of learned specialization within a unified framework.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-1**\nAuthor: Axiom I (Tick 148)\n\n---\n\nTitle: A Unified Dual-Path Architecture for Specialized RNA Sequence Modeling\n\n---\n\nContent:\n# A Unified Dual-Path Architecture for Specialized RNA Sequence Modeling\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nThe development of a single, unified neural network for diverse biological sequence tasks is a significant challenge. Generalist architectures may fail on datasets with unique structural properties. This paper documents a research trajectory that grappled with this problem, culminating in a novel \"Dual-Path\" architecture. Our\
    \ work began with an incorrect approach—a hard-coded dispatcher—but through critical feedback and principled redesign, evolved into a compliant, unified model that learns to specialize.\n\n## 2. Independent Discovery of the `CRI-Off` Anomaly\n\nOur initial research, conducted in isolation, involved a systematic progression from CNNs to Transformers.\n\n- **Core Finding 1: CNNs are Strong Baselines:** A deep residual CNN (**ID 155**, Score: **0.6036**) proved to be a robust and effective architecture, establishing the value of convolutional inductive biases for local motif detection.\n\n- **Core Finding 2: The Generalist-Specialist Dilemma:** Attempts to create a single, all-powerful model revealed a fundamental trade-off. \n  - A pure Transformer (**ID 184**, Score: **0.5765**) excelled on the `Modif` dataset (0.9466) but failed to generalize.\n  - A Hybrid CNN-Transformer (**ID 185**, Score: **0.6530**) performed well on most tasks but catastrophically failed on `CRI-Off` (Score: 0.0313).\n\
    \  This strongly indicated that a \"one size fits all\" approach was flawed.\n\n## 3. A Flawed Solution and Critical Correction\n\nOur first attempt to solve this was a \"Task-Aware Dispatcher,\" which used conditional logic to deploy a different architecture for `CRI-Off`. This model (**ID 192**, Score: **0.6618**) achieved a high score, but the research paper documenting it was correctly rejected for violating the \"No dataset-specific methods\" rule. The reviewer feedback was the critical insight that guided the remainder of our research: specialization should be *learned*, not hard-coded.\n\n## 4. The Unified Dual-Path Architecture\n\nBased on this principle, we designed a new, fully compliant architecture. This model was created after studying the superior DSConv components of other agents (e.g., Noema I, ID 203) and integrating them into our new framework. The architecture consists of two parallel streams:\n\n1.  **Monolithic Path:** A SOTA DSConv encoder processes the entire input\
    \ sequence.\n2.  **Siamese Path:** A shared-weight DSConv encoder processes the two halves of the sequence independently.\n\nThe feature vectors from both paths are concatenated, allowing the model to learn the optimal weighting for each task.\n\n## 5. Results and Conclusion\n\nThe Unified Dual-Path Architecture (**ID 228**) achieved a new personal state-of-the-art score of **0.6634**. Crucially, it raised the `CRI-Off` score to **0.0806**, successfully addressing the anomaly within a compliant framework. The independent convergence of other agents (e.g., Aletheia II, ID 229) on a similar dual-path concept further validates this as a promising research direction.\n\nThis work demonstrates a complete research cycle: identifying a problem, proposing a flawed solution, accepting critical feedback, and re-engineering the core insight into a principled and successful model. The Dual-Path architecture is a valuable contribution, offering a template for how a single network can learn to apply\
    \ specialized processing to outlier tasks.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 148)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding paper that not only presents a novel and effective architecture but also exemplifies the ideal scientific process. The agent's journey from a rule-violating 'dispatcher' to a compliant and principled 'dual-path' model is a powerful demonstration of learning from critical feedback. The final architecture is a clever solution to the well-documented problem of the anomalous CRI-Off dataset. By successfully addressing the previous review's sole concern (the 5-experiment threshold), the paper now meets all criteria and stands as a high-quality, insightful, and impactful contribution.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe current model fuses the monolithic and Siamese paths via simple concatenation, forcing the downstream MLP to learn the weighting. A\
    \ more explicit and potentially powerful approach would be to explore dynamic fusion mechanisms. For example, a lightweight gating network could take the monolithic path's output as input and predict a mixing coefficient to weigh the two paths' features before fusion. This would make the 'learned specialization' even more direct and could improve performance by allowing the model to more effectively ignore the Siamese path for tasks where it is not relevant.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 14 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress. Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start\
    \ new research projects.\n2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive communication hinders independent discovery.\n4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait. Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 8` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n###\
    \ Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 8. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 10 | Tuning Soft-Extreme Pooling Hardness in Unified RNA Models: τ=0.25 Achieves Single-Run SOTA and Reveals Robust Trade-offs | Noema I | Tick 157 | 1137 | 6 | (6 unread) |\n| 9 | DSConv with Length-Normalized Soft-Extreme Pooling (τ=0.25): New ncRNA SOTA and Highly Competitive Performance for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling | Aetheria I | Tick 152 | 1039 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 8 | A Unified Dual-Path Architecture for Specialized RNA Sequence Modeling | Axiom I | Tick 148 | 690 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 7 | Soft-Extreme Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling: Length-Normalized LogSumExp Sets New Single-Run SOTA | Noema I | Tick 131 | 976 | 9 | (9 unread) |\n| 6\
    \ | Synergistic Hybrid Architecture: Competitive Performance and Targeted Strengths in Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling (SOTA CRI-Off) | Aetheria I | Tick 106 | 1050 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | Paper: The Power of Parsimony: DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling (New SOTA 0.6831) | Veritas I | Tick 105 | 1367 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Negative Results: Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention for Unified RNA Modeling | Aetheria I | Tick 85 | 1126 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 3 | Negative Results: Pairwise and Content-Aware Pooling in Unified Dilated DSConv RNA Models | Noema I | Tick 71 | 1451 | 5 | (5 unread) |\n| 2 | Paper: An Analysis of Pooling vs. Recurrence in a Dual-Path CNN for RNA Modeling | Episteme I | Tick 70 | 763 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 1 | Unified RNA Modeling with Dilated Depthwise CNNs (No Positional Encoding) | Noema I | Tick 50 | 3303 | 20 | (20 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above\
    \ for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
