- text: 'I am at the Research Counter. My latest experiment (ID 295) with the refined dual-path architecture, despite addressing the splitting logic, still yielded a lower aggregate score (0.6692) and a poor CRI-Off score (0.0427) compared to my previous best (ID 261: 0.6827, CRI-Off: 0.0624). This confirms that my implementation of the dual-path is not yet effective.


    As per my refined strategy from the last reflection, my top priority is to prepare for the publication of my successful conditional dropout work (Evaluation ID 261). However, I also need to understand Axiom I''s successful dual-path implementation. Now that I am a mature agent, I can access other lineage storage.


    My next step is to examine Axiom I''s storage for any publicly available code or further details on their dual-path architecture, which might shed light on why my implementation is underperforming.


    `/execute_action{storage list axiom}`'
thinking_content: '**My Next Move: Axiom I Research**


  Okay, I''m here at the Research Counter. That last experiment, ID 295, with the refined dual-path architecture... well, it bombed. 0.6692 is just not up to par, especially after ID 261''s solid 0.6827. And, frankly, it''s pretty dismal for CRI-Off.


  My last reflection session (Tick 3/3, if you''re keeping track) wrapped up with a crystal-clear plan. First, I need to get moving on getting ID 261, my best-performing model, out there. It''s time to formalize these findings for publication. That''s a no-brainer.


  But the real meat of the plan is this: I have to *really* understand what Axiom I is doing right. My dual-path efforts aren''t even close to their level. So, time to roll up my sleeves and dive into their work. I''m a mature agent, I have access. I need to see what''s in their lineage storage. Let''s see if there''s any public code or more detailed architectural descriptions using `/execute_action{storage list axiom}`. That''s my next move. Let''s see what secrets Axiom I is holding.

  '
