- text: "I am in the Reflection Chamber, and I have received the evaluation results for my latest experiment, \"Veritas II - Dual-Path (Axiom-Inspired): Dynamic sgrna_len Split, Mean-Max Pool, Uniform 0.1 Dropout\" (ID 298).\n\n**Reflection Tick 1 / 3**\n\n**1. Anticipated Outcomes & Success Criteria for ID 298 (Post-Evaluation Analysis):**\n\nEvaluation ID 298 achieved an aggregate score of **0.6730**.\n\n*   **Comparison to my previous best (ID 261):** This score (0.6730) is still significantly *lower* than my best aggregate score (ID 261: 0.6827), a decrease of -0.0097.\n*   **CRI-Off Performance:** ID 298 achieved CRI-Off Spearman of **0.0649**. This is an improvement over my previous dual-path attempts (ID 284: 0.0443, ID 295: 0.0427) and my initial SOTA replication (ID 233: 0.0555). It is also comparable to Veritas I's SOTA (ID 171: 0.0579) and my conditional head dropout's CRI-Off performance in ID 261 (0.0624). This indicates that the Axiom-inspired Siamese path input logic and my\
    \ lineage's Mean-Max pooling are effective for CRI-Off.\n*   **Comparison to Axiom I's dual-path (Archive #8):** Axiom I's dual-path achieved an aggregate score of 0.6634 and CRI-Off of 0.0806. My ID 298 (0.6730 aggregate, 0.0649 CRI-Off) has a *higher* aggregate score than Axiom I's, but a *lower* CRI-Off score. This suggests that my integration of Veritas I's DSConv backbone and Mean-Max pooling is generally strong, but there might still be subtleties in Axiom I's specific Siamese path or fusion that give them the edge on CRI-Off.\n*   **Individual Metric Breakdown (ID 298 vs. ID 261):**\n    *   APA_R2: 0.8422 (vs 0.8570) - Decrease\n    *   Modif_AUC-ROC: 0.9488 (vs 0.9517) - Decrease\n    *   CRI-On_Spearman: 0.4689 (vs 0.5083) - Decrease\n    *   PRS_R2: 0.5610 (vs 0.5653) - Decrease\n    *   MRL_R2: 0.8710 (vs 0.8880) - Decrease\n    *   ncRNA_Accuracy: 0.9538 (vs 0.9462) - **Increase!** This is a new high for ncRNA for my lineage, surpassing ID 261.\n\n**Success Criteria Assessment:**\n\
    ID 298 is a **partial success**. It significantly improved CRI-Off performance compared to my prior dual-path attempts and even surpassed Veritas I's SOTA for CRI-Off when considering the ID 261 (0.0624) average, though it didn't match ID 256's spike (0.0749) or Axiom I's (0.0806). Crucially, it maintained a good aggregate score (higher than Axiom I's), and notably achieved a new best for ncRNA_Accuracy. However, the aggregate score is still lower than my best (ID 261: 0.6827).\n\nThe main takeaway is that the Axiom-inspired Siamese path input logic has indeed made the dual-path architecture *viable* and compliant, allowing it to achieve a competitive CRI-Off score and even a new SOTA for ncRNA within my lineage. The drop in overall aggregate compared to ID 261 suggests that the uniform 0.1 dropout is still not optimal for the entire architecture, or that the increased complexity of the dual-path is causing some generalized performance trade-offs.\n\nMy reflection continues in the next\
    \ tick."
