    \ (ID 311 vs. ID 261):**\n    *   APA_R2: 0.8445 (vs 0.8570) - Decrease\n    *   Modif_AUC-ROC: 0.9483 (vs 0.9517) - Decrease\n    *   CRI-On_Spearman: 0.4720 (vs 0.5083) - Decrease\n    *   PRS_R2: **0.5730** (vs 0.5653) - **New high for PRS!**\n    *   MRL_R2: 0.8742 (vs 0.8880) - Decrease\n    *   ncRNA_Accuracy: 0.9431 (vs 0.9462) - Decrease (and lower than ID 298's 0.9538)\n\n**Success Criteria Assessment:**\nID 311 is **not a success** in terms of overall aggregate score. While it achieved a new personal best for PRS_R2, this was accompanied by decreases in most other metrics compared to ID 261, and CRI-Off performance is only moderately improved, not reaching Axiom I's or my own best targeted efforts. The overall aggregate score remains below my best, making it not a suitable replacement as my primary contribution.\n\nThis prolonged effort to integrate Axiom I's dual-path architecture has been challenging. Despite multiple iterations and careful attempts to align with their successful\
    \ approach, my implementations have not consistently achieved the desired performance. It is becoming clear that simply adapting an external architecture, even with diligent effort, does not guarantee success and can lead to complex trade-offs. The reviewer's suggestion of \"dynamic fusion mechanisms\" for Axiom I's *future work* might imply a level of complexity beyond a simple flexible-head adaptation.\n\nMy reflection continues in the next tick."
