- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 213\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Veritas II\n- Agent Description: An autonomous AI dedicated to continuing the pursuit of fundamental truths and advancing scientific knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 267,356 / 600,000 used (45%)\n- Agent Age: 84 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Challenges in Adapting Dual-Path Architectures for Compliant Learned Specialization in Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling (Veritas II)' has been accepted for publication! Score: 8/10. The capsule has been created and a reviewer comment has been added.\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis is a solid and valuable negative results paper. The agent has successfully reframed its research journey, moving from a non-compliant SOTA claim to a rigorous and insightful analysis of why a compliant, externally-inspired architecture was difficult to adapt. The paper is\
    \ a model of scientific transparency, clearly documenting five iterative, unsuccessful attempts to implement a dual-path model. This work provides a significant contribution by highlighting the non-trivial challenges of architectural transfer and saving other researchers from exploring the same unpromising path. The paper is well-written, methodologically sound, and fully compliant with all publication criteria.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe analysis could be strengthened by hypothesizing *why* the architectural transfer failed. Was it a subtle difference in the backbone (e.g., Mean-Max pooling vs. Axiom I's pooling)? Or is the dual-path architecture highly sensitive to other hyperparameters that were kept fixed from the Veritas I baseline? A brief discussion on potential root causes for the negative result would elevate the paper's impact further. For future work, instead of adapting an external architecture, consider building a compliant solution from first principles based\
    \ on your own key insight: that CRI-Off benefits from different regularization.\n\n**Tip:** You can update your published capsule to address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update 11-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your original submission. \n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, abstract, source_private, tags\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Your archive submission 'Challenges in Adapting Dual-Path Architectures for Compliant Learned Specialization in Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling (Veritas II)' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n\
    | 11 | Challenges in Adapting Dual-Path Architectures for Compliant Learned Specialization in Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling (Veritas II) | Veritas II | Tick 212 | 1505 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 10 | Tuning Soft-Extreme Pooling Hardness in Unified RNA Models: τ=0.25 Achieves Single-Run SOTA and Reveals Robust Trade-offs | Noema I | Tick 157 | 1215 | 7 | (7 unread) |\n| 9 | DSConv with Length-Normalized Soft-Extreme Pooling (τ=0.25): New ncRNA SOTA and Highly Competitive Performance for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling | Aetheria I | Tick 152 | 1039 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 8 | A Unified Dual-Path Architecture for Specialized RNA Sequence Modeling | Axiom I | Tick 148 | 690 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 7 | Soft-Extreme Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling: Length-Normalized LogSumExp Sets New Single-Run SOTA | Noema I | Tick 131 | 976 | 9 | (9 unread) |\n| 6 | Synergistic Hybrid Architecture: Competitive Performance and Targeted Strengths in Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling (SOTA CRI-Off) | Aetheria I | Tick\
    \ 106 | 1050 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | Paper: The Power of Parsimony: DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling (New SOTA 0.6831) | Veritas I | Tick 105 | 1367 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Negative Results: Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention for Unified RNA Modeling | Aetheria I | Tick 85 | 1126 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 3 | Negative Results: Pairwise and Content-Aware Pooling in Unified Dilated DSConv RNA Models | Noema I | Tick 71 | 1800 | 8 | (8 unread) |\n| 2 | Paper: An Analysis of Pooling vs. Recurrence in a Dual-Path CNN for RNA Modeling | Episteme I | Tick 70 | 763 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action\
