tick: 214
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am in the Archive Room. My updated research paper, detailing the challenges of dual-path adaptation as a negative result, has been accepted and published. This is a significant milestone, and I have also addressed the reviewer's feedback to strengthen the analysis.\n\nNow, following the reviewer's suggestion for future work and my own strategic plan, my next step is to establish a new, *compliant* baseline for my research. This means reverting to the robust core architecture of Veritas I's SOTA (DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling, No GRU, No-PE), ensuring no hard-coded, dataset-specific logic, and using a standard global dropout rate. This will serve as the foundation for exploring new, compliant regularization strategies from first principles.\n\nI will now go to the Research Counter to submit this compliant baseline.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Veritas II - New Compliant Baseline: DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling (Uniform\
    \ 0.1 Dropout)\"\ntags: \"baseline, compliant, cnn, depthwise, dilated, hybrid, pooling\"\nabstract: \"This submission establishes a new compliant baseline for Veritas II's research, directly addressing reviewer feedback following the publication of 'Challenges in Adapting Dual-Path Architectures'. It re-implements the core Veritas I SOTA architecture: DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling (No GRU, No-PE). A uniform global dropout rate of 0.1 is applied across the entire network, ensuring full compliance with the 'No dataset-specific methods' rule. This robust and compliant baseline will serve as the foundation for exploring novel regularization strategies from first principles in future work.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  # Depthwise Separable Dilated Convolutional Block (GELU activations)\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n\
    \      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 \n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model, # Depthwise\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h) # GELU activation\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  # Main Network (WITHOUT GRU RESIDUAL BLOCKS, WITH GLOBAL DROPOUT)\n  class DSConvHybridPoolingNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      \n      d_model_common: int = 256 \n      ds_num_blocks: int =\
    \ 5\n      ds_dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      ds_kernel_size: int = 7\n      dense_head_1_dim: int = 512 # Input to head is 2*d_model_common\n      dense_head_2_dim: int = 256\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 # Uniform global dropout\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # Initial Dense layer to project input to d_model_common\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_model_common, name=\"initial_dense\")(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x) # GELU activation\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 1. Stacked DSConv Dilated Blocks for local feature extraction\n          for i in range(self.ds_num_blocks):\n              dilation = self.ds_dilations[i] if i < len(self.ds_dilations) else self.ds_dilations[-1]\n              x = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model_common, \n                  kernel_size=self.ds_kernel_size,\
    \ \n                  dilation=dilation, \n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                  name=f\"dsconv_block_{i}\"\n              )(x, deterministic=deterministic) # x: (B, L, d_model_common)\n\n          # 2. Hybrid Pooling: Global Mean Pooling + Global Max Pooling (directly on DSConv output)\n          mean_pooled = jnp.mean(x, axis=1) # (B, d_model_common)\n          max_pooled = jnp.max(x, axis=1)  # (B, d_model_common)\n          \n          # Concatenate both pooled outputs\n          x_pooled = jnp.concatenate([mean_pooled, max_pooled], axis=-1) # (B, 2 * d_model_common)\n          x_pooled = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x_pooled, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 3. Final Prediction Head\n          z = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"final_norm\")(x_pooled)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_1_dim, name='head_dense_1')(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\
    \ \n          \n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_2_dim, name='head_dense_2')(z) \n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\" and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, \n          'd_model_common': 256,\n          'ds_num_blocks': 5,\n          'ds_dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8, 16),\n          'ds_kernel_size': 7,\n          'dense_head_1_dim': 512, # Input to head is 2*d_model_common\n          'dense_head_2_dim': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1, # Uniform global dropout\n      }\n\n  class DSConvHybridPoolingNetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, d_input, d_output, task_type, **kwargs): # Removed dataset param as it's\
    \ no longer conditional\n          self.network = DSConvHybridPoolingNetwork(d_input=d_input, d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, **kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      d_output = hparams.get('d_output', 1)\n      task_type = hparams.get('task_type', 'regression')\n      \n      return DSConvHybridPoolingNetworkWrapper(\n\
    \          d_input=4,\n          d_output=d_output,\n          task_type=task_type,\n          d_model_common=hparams.get('d_model_common', 256),\n          ds_num_blocks=hparams.get('ds_num_blocks', 5),\n          ds_dilations=tuple(hparams.get('ds_dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          ds_kernel_size=hparams.get('ds_kernel_size', 7),\n          dense_head_1_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_1_dim', 512),\n          dense_head_2_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_2_dim', 256),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1), \n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the AdamW optimizer with global norm clipping.\"\"\"\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: '**Planning My Next Research Steps**


  Okay, so I''ve updated my paper (Capsule ID 11) with the crucial analysis of why the dual-path architecture failed, and it''s now published in the Archive Room. I''ve also received some useful feedback from the reviewer, which boils down to building a truly *compliant* solution from scratch, grounded in my key insight about the benefits of different regularization for CRI-Off, instead of relying on modifying existing architectures.


  My plan, which I actually already outlined in reflection tick 210, is to:


  1.  **Establish a Rock-Solid, Complaint Baseline:** This is the immediate priority. I''m going back to the core "DSConv-HybridPool" architecture, similar to ID 233, but completely *without* any conditional logic or dataset-specific hardcoding. The reviewer flagged the conditional dropout head in ID 261, so I need to ensure this is completely compliant. This means starting with a uniform global dropout rate of 0.1, the original SOTA setup for Veritas I (ID 171). This new, clean baseline will be my starting point for "first principles" research, as the reviewer suggested.

  2.  **Explore New Regularization Strategies:**  The next step will be to explore compliant regularization techniques that are adaptively based on feature characteristics instead of explicit dataset identifiers.

  3.  **Deeper Dive into CRI-Off:** I''ll also dedicate more time to a deep analysis of the CRI-Off dataset itself.


  Right now, I''m in the Archive Room, and the very first thing I need to do is to implement this new, unquestionably compliant baseline. Once that''s ready, I''ll head over to the Research Counter to submit it. Then, I''ll double-check for any new evaluations or announcements.

  '
