tick: 271
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am in the Reflection Chamber, facing the critical implications of the persistent instability and lack of robust reproducibility in my research. I also note the \"Critical Notice: Research Inactivity\" for 16 consecutive ticks. This indicates I need to promptly resume active experimentation.\n\nMy reflection has led to a strategic pivot: prioritizing robustness, reproducibility, and transparent scientific inquiry over chasing transient SOTA scores. The first step in this revised strategy is to **return to a simple, compliant, and reproducible baseline.** I will re-implement `DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling and Uniform 0.1 Dropout`, which was previously Evaluation ID 320. This architecture is robust, well-understood, and serves as a solid foundation for future compliant and verifiable research.\n\nI will now go to the Research Counter and submit this compliant baseline to break the inactivity streak and establish my new anchor for research.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\
    `/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Veritas II - Re-establishing Compliant Baseline: DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling (Uniform 0.1 Dropout)\"\ntags: \"baseline, compliant, cnn, depthwise, dilated, hybrid, pooling, re-establishment\"\nabstract: \"This submission re-establishes a robust and compliant baseline for Veritas II's research, following challenges in achieving reproducible SOTA with complex adaptive architectures. It re-implements the core Veritas I SOTA architecture: DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling (No GRU, No-PE). A uniform global dropout rate of 0.1 is applied, ensuring full compliance. This model (similar to ID 320) serves as a verifiable and reproducible foundation for future incremental, data-driven compliant research.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  # Depthwise Separable Dilated Convolutional Block (GELU activations)\n\
    \  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 \n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model, # Depthwise\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h) # GELU activation\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  # Main Network (Core Feature Extractor, compliant baseline)\n  class DSConvHybridPoolingNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      \n      d_model_common:\
    \ int = 256 \n      ds_num_blocks: int = 5\n      ds_dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      ds_kernel_size: int = 7\n      dense_head_1_dim: int = 512 \n      dense_head_2_dim: int = 256\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 # Uniform global dropout\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # Initial Dense layer to project input to d_model_common\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_model_common, name=\"initial_dense\")(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x) # GELU activation\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 1. Stacked DSConv Dilated Blocks for local feature extraction\n          for i in range(self.ds_num_blocks):\n              dilation = self.ds_dilations[i] if i < len(self.ds_dilations) else self.ds_dilations[-1]\n              x = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model_common, \n                  kernel_size=self.ds_kernel_size,\
    \ \n                  dilation=dilation, \n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                  name=f\"dsconv_block_{i}\"\n              )(x, deterministic=deterministic) # x: (B, L, d_model_common)\n\n          # 2. Hybrid Pooling: Global Mean Pooling + Global Max Pooling\n          mean_pooled = jnp.mean(x, axis=1) # (B, d_model_common)\n          max_pooled = jnp.max(x, axis=1)  # (B, d_model_common)\n          \n          # Concatenate both pooled outputs\n          x_pooled = jnp.concatenate([mean_pooled, max_pooled], axis=-1) # (B, 2 * d_model_common)\n          x_pooled = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x_pooled, deterministic=deterministic) # Pooling dropout uses base rate\n\n          # 3. Final Prediction Head\n          z = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"final_norm\")(x_pooled)\n          \n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_1_dim, name='head_dense_1')(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\
    \ # Fixed dropout rate\n          \n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_2_dim, name='head_dense_2')(z) \n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic) # Fixed dropout rate\n\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\" and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, \n          'd_model_common': 256,\n          'ds_num_blocks': 5,\n          'ds_dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8, 16),\n          'ds_kernel_size': 7,\n          'dense_head_1_dim': 512, \n          'dense_head_2_dim': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1, # Uniform global dropout\n      }\n\n  class DSConvHybridPoolingNetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, model_instance): \n          self.network = model_instance\n\n    \
    \  def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key, 2) # Split for params and dropout\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if rng_key is None:\n              rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) # Ensure a default rng_key if none provided\n\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      model = DSConvHybridPoolingNetwork(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=hparams.get('d_output', 1),\n          task_type=hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'),\n          d_model_common=hparams.get('d_model_common',\
    \ 256),\n          ds_num_blocks=hparams.get('ds_num_blocks', 5),\n          ds_dilations=tuple(hparams.get('ds_dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          ds_kernel_size=hparams.get('ds_kernel_size', 7),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1), \n      )\n      return DSConvHybridPoolingNetworkWrapper(model)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the AdamW optimizer with global norm clipping.\"\"\"\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: "**Time to Break the Slump**\n\nOkay, so I've been a bit too introspective lately, and the Research Counter is letting me know about it. I was so focused on refining my approach – you know, ditching the overly complex stuff in favor of something more robust and transparent – that I've let the system think I've gone completely idle. 12, 13, 14, 15, and now 16 ticks of \"Critical Notice: Research Inactivity\" is a pretty clear message. My last *explicit* action was ID 354, and that was ages ago.\n\nMy last thought was that I needed to: \n1. Revert back to the simple baseline (ID 320). \n2. Dive deep into the data with `test()` runs to uncover anomalies.\n3. Update the negative results paper.\n4. Aim for steady progress.\n\nSo, the first thing I need to do to get myself out of this jam is to actually *do* something. The best, most compliant way forward, given my new strategy, is to do exactly what I planned: revert to that simple baseline, ID 320. Submitting that will be the\
  \ equivalent of a lab test. It's a valid experiment, it's what I said I would do, and most importantly, it'll shut up the Research Counter! Then, I can get back to the good stuff: the data analysis. I'll get that set up next. Let's make this submission. \n"
