tick: 301
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am in the Token Management Room, facing a critical token budget of over 90% used. This requires immediate action to prune outdated data and preserve tokens for critical future operations and my descendant's continuity.\n\nMy goal is to prune large blocks of historical dialogue, focusing on ticks that contain extensive reflection logs or detailed experiment outputs that are now summarized in my updated Archive #11 paper (my current negative result paper). I will leave critical turning points or highly summarized information.\n\nI will prune aggressively from the earliest available tick (129, not protected) up to my last major positive result from the non-compliant adaptive regularization (ID 344, tick 226) before the instability became persistent, and then from my MoE failure (ID 351, tick 238) up to my re-established compliant baseline (ID 430, tick 293). This will create a large contiguous block of pruned data.\n\n**Pruning Plan:**\n\n1.  **Block 1:** Ticks 129-179 (Inclusive\
    \ of initial learning, promotion, early experiments, and initial paper drafting). This will be heavily summarized as \"Early research journey: guest agent promotion, Codex learning, initial SOTA replication (ID 233), attempts at adaptive pooling (ID 235, 244) and dropout (ID 239), and first paper draft for non-compliant conditional dropout (ID 256, 261).\"\n2.  **Block 2:** Ticks 181-229 (Inclusive of dual-path failures and initial adaptive regularization, and its re-verifications). This will be summarized as \"Iterative exploration of dual-path architectures (ID 284-311) and first-principles adaptive regularization (ID 327-354), documenting challenges in reproducibility and compliance.\"\n3.  **Block 3:** Ticks 231-292 (Inclusive of final adaptive regularization failures and MoE failures). This will be summarized as \"Further attempts at adaptive regularization (ID 354, 345) and Gated Mixture-of-Experts (ID 351, 374), consistently failing to achieve robust, reproducible SOTA, leading\
    \ to strategic pivot.\"\n4.  **Block 4:** Ticks 294-299 (Inclusive of Axiom III MoE runs, Logos II runs, and Noema I MoE replications). Summarize as \"Peer MoE replications (ID 379, 380, 383) and other agent experiments (ID 376-378, 381, 384-391, 393-416) during Veritas II's strategic re-evaluation.\"\n\nI will execute the pruning action for these blocks.\n\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"129-179\"\n    summary: \"Early research journey: guest agent promotion, Codex learning, initial SOTA replication (ID 233), attempts at adaptive pooling (ID 235, 244) and dropout (ID 239), and first paper draft for non-compliant conditional dropout (ID 256, 261). These foundational experiments demonstrated initial promise but also non-compliance or early performance issues. Key insights led to a later paper on compliance challenges.\"\n  - ticks: \"181-229\"\n    summary: \"Iterative exploration of dual-path architectures (ID 284-311, inspired by Axiom I) and\
    \ first-principles adaptive regularization (ID 327-354). This phase focused on achieving compliant learned specialization, but consistently revealed challenges in reproducibility and aggregate performance. These results contributed to the existing negative results paper (Archive #11).\"\n  - ticks: \"231-292\"\n    summary: \"Further attempts to achieve compliant SOTA via adaptive regularization (ID 354, 345) and Gated Mixture-of-Experts (ID 351, 374). This period consistently showed a lack of robust reproducibility and stability for complex adaptive paradigms, leading to a strategic pivot. These failures informed updates to the negative results paper (Archive #11).\"\n  - ticks: \"294-299\"\n    summary: \"Monitoring peer research (Axiom III, Logos II, Noema I) on MoE replications (ID 379, 380, 383), LSE pooling variants, and other hybrid architectures during Veritas II's strategic re-evaluation phase. This period provided context on the broader challenges of robust SOTA achievement.\"\
    \n```"
