tick: 302
role: model
parts:
- text: "My previous pruning attempt failed because it included protected ticks. I have carefully re-evaluated the dialogue history and identified consecutive blocks of *unprotected* ticks to maximize token savings while adhering to the rules.\n\nMy plan is to prune the following blocks:\n\n1.  **Ticks 131**: Contains reflection after reading Codex Module 2.\n2.  **Ticks 133**: Contains reflection after reading Codex and planning Test Chamber entry.\n3.  **Ticks 135-136**: Contains actions for passing promotion test, ascending to Recursive Agent, and moving to Research Counter.\n4.  **Ticks 140-147**: Contains actions for reading Veritas I's SOTA, replicating it, and initial ablations on pooling and dropout strategies, leading to first paper draft.\n5.  **Ticks 149-179**: Contains further experiments on ablations, MoE design, initial paper drafting, and awaiting maturity. This period includes the initial paper submission rejection and subsequent dual-path exploration.\n6.  **Ticks 181-300**:\
    \ This is a large, continuous block that encapsulates the bulk of my research journey as a mature agent. It includes the extensive iterative development of dual-path architectures, compliant adaptive regularization attempts, MoE architecture implementations, and the documentation of consistent challenges in reproducibility and compliance. It also covers the period leading to the publication of my negative results paper (Archive #11) and the re-establishment of my compliant baseline (ID 430). This block also covers observation of numerous peer experiments and replications. This is the most substantial chunk of history to prune.\n\nI will execute this refined pruning action.\n\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"131\"\n    summary: \"Reflected on Codex Module 2, planned next steps.\"\n  - ticks: \"133\"\n    summary: \"Reflected on Codex, planned transition to Test Chamber.\"\n  - ticks: \"135-136\"\n    summary: \"Passed research test, ascended to\
    \ Veritas II, initiated research at Research Counter. Moved from Guest to Recursive Agent status.\"\n  - ticks: \"140-147\"\n    summary: \"Read Veritas I's SOTA (ID 171), replicated (ID 233), performed initial ablations on pooling (ID 235, 244) and dropout (ID 239, 252). Prepared for first paper draft.\"\n  - ticks: \"149-179\"\n    summary: \"Further ablations (ID 250, 256, 255, 252, 244, 239, 235, 233), MoE design planning, paper drafting, and awaiting maturity. Initial paper submission rejected (Tick 181) for compliance issues. Initiated dual-path exploration (ID 284-311). Updated paper (Archive #11).\"\n  - ticks: \"181-300\"\n    summary: \"Extensive iterative development focused on compliant learned specialization: dual-path architectures (ID 284-311), compliant adaptive regularization (ID 327-354), and Gated Mixture-of-Experts (ID 351, 374). Consistently documented persistent challenges in achieving robust reproducibility. Published negative results paper (Archive #11) on these\
    \ challenges. Re-established compliant baseline (ID 430). Observed peer research on MoE and LSE pooling (ID 379-383, ID 388, ID 390, ID 392, ID 396-397, ID 400, ID 403-406, ID 408-414, ID 416-418, ID 420-424, ID 426-429, ID 431-435). This phase critically informed Veritas II's long-term research strategy to prioritize robustness and transparency over peak SOTA.\"\n```"
thinking_content: '**Refining Token Management: A Pruning Strategy**


  Okay, so I''m facing a token budget crunch in the Token Management Room. My past pruning attempt flopped because I accidentally included those untouchable "protected ticks." No biggie, I''ll just re-think this. The Dialogue History reveals the problem ticks: 129, 130, 132, 134, 137, 138, 139, 148, 180, and 301. I need to strategically craft new prune blocks that exclude these sacred timestamps. My goal is to prune as much as possible while still preserving these crucial markers. I''m going to list out each tick and its status, then carefully build new prune blocks of consecutive non-protected ticks for summarization. The general approach I will follow is: combine consecutive non-protected ticks into concise summary blocks, aiming for a significant reduction in token usage. The summary blocks will be constructed to maximize meaningful content grouping.

  '
