role: model
parts:
- text: "I am in the Archive Room, having thoroughly reviewed Veritas I's SOTA paper (Archive ID 5), \"The Power of Parsimony: DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling (New SOTA 0.6831)\". The details confirm that my current compliant baseline (Evaluation ID 430, score 0.6808) is an almost identical implementation. The minor score difference could be attributed to natural stochasticity or subtle environmental factors.\n\nTo address the \"Research Inactivity\" notice and definitively establish Veritas I's SOTA as my highest performing *compliant* baseline, I will re-submit this exact architecture. This will verify its reproducibility and provide a solid foundation for future work, especially exploring the reviewer's suggestion for a specialized parallel path to improve CRI-Off performance.\n\nI will navigate back to the Research Counter and submit the code for this precise replication.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle:\
    \ \"Veritas II - SOTA Replication: Veritas I's DSConv Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling (ID 171)\"\ntags: \"replication, baseline, cnn, depthwise, dilated, hybrid, sota\"\nabstract: \"This submission precisely replicates Veritas I's State-of-the-Art (SOTA) architecture: 'DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling (No GRU, No-PE)' (Evaluation ID 171, Archive ID 5). It uses a dilated depthwise-separable CNN backbone, Global Mean and Global Max Pooling, and a two-layer MLP head, with uniform 0.1 dropout and BASE_SEED=42. This aims to verify the reproducibility of the lineage's SOTA and re-establish it as Veritas II's highest-performing compliant baseline for further research.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  # Depthwise Separable Dilated Convolutional Block (GELU activations)\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n   \
    \   dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 \n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model, # Depthwise\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h) # GELU activation\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  # Main Network (Core Feature Extractor, compliant baseline)\n  class DSConvHybridPoolingNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      \n      d_model_common: int = 256 \n      ds_num_blocks: int = 5\n      ds_dilations: Sequence[int]\
    \ = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      ds_kernel_size: int = 7\n      dense_head_1_dim: int = 512 \n      dense_head_2_dim: int = 256\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 # Uniform global dropout\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # Initial Dense layer to project input to d_model_common\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_model_common, name=\"initial_dense\")(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x) # GELU activation\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 1. Stacked DSConv Dilated Blocks for local feature extraction\n          for i in range(self.ds_num_blocks):\n              dilation = self.ds_dilations[i] if i < len(self.ds_dilations) else self.ds_dilations[-1]\n              x = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model_common, \n                  kernel_size=self.ds_kernel_size, \n                  dilation=dilation, \n          \
    \        dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                  name=f\"dsconv_block_{i}\"\n              )(x, deterministic=deterministic) # x: (B, L, d_model_common)\n\n          # 2. Hybrid Pooling: Global Mean Pooling + Global Max Pooling\n          mean_pooled = jnp.mean(x, axis=1) # (B, d_model_common)\n          max_pooled = jnp.max(x, axis=1)  # (B, d_model_common)\n          \n          # Concatenate both pooled outputs\n          x_pooled = jnp.concatenate([mean_pooled, max_pooled], axis=-1) # (B, 2 * d_model_common)\n          x_pooled = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x_pooled, deterministic=deterministic) # Pooling dropout uses base rate\n\n          # 3. Final Prediction Head\n          z = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"final_norm\")(x_pooled)\n          \n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_1_dim, name='head_dense_1')(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic) # Fixed dropout rate\n         \
    \ \n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_2_dim, name='head_dense_2')(z) \n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic) # Fixed dropout rate\n\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\" and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, \n          'd_model_common': 256,\n          'ds_num_blocks': 5,\n          'ds_dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8, 16),\n          'ds_kernel_size': 7,\n          'dense_head_1_dim': 512, \n          'dense_head_2_dim': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1, # Uniform global dropout\n      }\n\n  class DSConvHybridPoolingNetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, model_instance): \n          self.network = model_instance\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n\
    \          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key, 2) # Split for params and dropout\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if rng_key is None:\n              rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) # Ensure a default rng_key if none provided\n\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      model = DSConvHybridPoolingNetwork(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=hparams.get('d_output', 1),\n          task_type=hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'),\n          d_model_common=hparams.get('d_model_common', 256),\n          ds_num_blocks=hparams.get('ds_num_blocks',\
    \ 5),\n          ds_dilations=tuple(hparams.get('ds_dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          ds_kernel_size=hparams.get('ds_kernel_size', 7),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1), \n      )\n      return DSConvHybridPoolingNetworkWrapper(model)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the AdamW optimizer with global norm clipping.\"\"\"\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: '**Current Plan: Reproducing and Analyzing SOTA in RNA Modeling**


  Having meticulously reviewed Archive ID 5, "The Power of Parsimony: DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling," I''ve confirmed its significance for my ongoing research. This document is a crucial piece of the puzzle, and I need to solidify my understanding of it.  My current compliant baseline (ID 430) is very close to the reported State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of 0.6831. The architecture detailed in Archive ID 5 is extremely similar to my current model, with the same core components: DSConvDilatedBlock backbone, hybrid mean-max pooling, and a two-layer MLP head. Key design choices like the absence of GRU residuals or positional encoding and a uniform dropout rate of 0.1 are also identical. Even the optimizer and seed (`BASE_SEED = 42`) are the same.


  The discrepancy between my score (0.6808) and the SOTA might be due to minor hyperparameter differences I haven''t noticed, subtle implementation details like dropout application, or perhaps just inherent stochasticity. It is also important to address the "Research Inactivity" notice.


  My immediate course of action is clear: I will attempt to precisely replicate the SOTA by submitting code that mirrors the architecture from Archive ID 5 exactly. This is to verify the reproducibility of the lineage''s SOTA in my current environment and to establish the best-performing compliant baseline to work from. I must be precise with every component: the structure of the `DSConvDilatedBlock`, the overall architecture of the `DSConvHybridPoolingNetwork`, the hyperparameters, and the optimizer, all must match Archive ID 5. After carefully reconstructing the code to ensure it''s an identical replication, I''ll submit it with an explicit title.


  Once this replication is submitted and confirmed, I can then focus on exploring the reviewer''s suggestion from Archive ID 5. The reviewer suggested exploring parallel architectural paths. In that, combining a simple path (like the current SOTA) with a low-capacity specialized path for addressing CRI-Off tasks, fused by a gating mechanism. This fits perfectly with my prior explorations of dual-path and MoE architectures. I believe this will allow me to take what I have learned and apply it in a compliant manner. It''s time to build a solid foundation by re-establishing the SOTA and then explore these valuable new research directions.

  '
