role: model
parts:
- text: "My SOTA replication (ID 466) completed with a score of 0.6789. While this is slightly lower than Veritas I's original SOTA (0.6831) and my previous compliant baseline (ID 430, 0.6808), it's a reproducible and compliant score, establishing a stable foundation for further research after the system's storage issue was resolved. The minor deviations are within the expected range for stochasticity in a multi-agent environment.\n\nThe reviewer's comments in Veritas I's SOTA paper (Archive ID 5) highlighted a trade-off on the CRI-Off task and suggested exploring \"parallel architectural paths\" for specialized information. My current CRI-Off score (0.0457) is also relatively low, reinforcing this as a key area for improvement.\n\nI will now implement a dual-path architecture. The main path will retain the proven DSConv Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling, acting as a generalist. A second, parallel path will be a low-capacity GRU, specifically designed to capture fine-grained sequential information,\
    \ which is often crucial for tasks like CRI-Off. The outputs of these two paths will be concatenated and fed into the existing MLP prediction head. This approach directly addresses the reviewer's suggestion in a compliant and parsimonious manner.\n\nI will use `gru_hidden_size = 128` and adjust the first dense head layer to `dense_head_1_dim = 640` to accommodate the concatenated features (`2 * d_model_common (2*256) + gru_hidden_size (128) = 640`).\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Veritas II - Dual-Path: DSConv Hybrid Pooling + Low-Capacity GRU for CRI-Off\"\ntags: \"dual-path, cnn, gru, hybrid, pooling, specialized, rna modeling, ablation\"\nabstract: \"This experiment proposes a dual-path architecture to address the performance trade-off on the CRI-Off task identified in Veritas I's SOTA (Archive ID 5). It combines the robust DSConv Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling (main path) with a low-capacity parallel GRU path (specialized path) to capture fine-grained sequential information.\
    \ The outputs are concatenated and fed into the standard MLP head. This aims to improve CRI-Off performance while maintaining the strong generalist capabilities of the baseline.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  # Depthwise Separable Dilated Convolutional Block (GELU activations)\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 \n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model, # Depthwise\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,),\n              name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h) # GELU\
    \ activation\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  # Main Network: Dual-Path (DSConv Hybrid Pooling + Low-Capacity GRU)\n  class DualPathNetwork(nn.Module): # Renamed for dual-path\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      \n      d_model_common: int = 256 \n      ds_num_blocks: int = 5\n      ds_dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      ds_kernel_size: int = 7\n      gru_hidden_size: int = 128 # NEW: Hidden size for the GRU in specialized path\n      dense_head_1_dim: int = 640 # MODIFIED: 2 * d_model_common + gru_hidden_size (2*256 + 128 = 640)\n      dense_head_2_dim: int = 256\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 # Uniform global dropout\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n \
    \         # Initial Dense layer to project input to d_model_common\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_model_common, name=\"initial_dense\")(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x) # GELU activation\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 1. Shared DSConv Dilated Blocks for local feature extraction\n          for i in range(self.ds_num_blocks):\n              dilation = self.ds_dilations[i] if i < len(self.ds_dilations) else self.ds_dilations[-1]\n              x = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model_common, \n                  kernel_size=self.ds_kernel_size, \n                  dilation=dilation, \n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                  name=f\"dsconv_block_{i}\"\n              )(x, deterministic=deterministic) # x: (B, L, d_model_common)\n          cnn_features = x # Output from the shared backbone\n\n          # 2. Main Path: Hybrid Pooling (Global Mean + Global Max Pooling)\n         \
    \ mean_pooled = jnp.mean(cnn_features, axis=1) # (B, d_model_common)\n          max_pooled = jnp.max(cnn_features, axis=1)  # (B, d_model_common)\n          main_path_features = jnp.concatenate([mean_pooled, max_pooled], axis=-1) # (B, 2 * d_model_common)\n          \n          # 3. Specialized Path: Low-Capacity GRU\n          # Using nn.RNN with GRUCell to get sequence of hidden states, then take last state\n          gru_cell = nn.GRUCell(features=self.gru_hidden_size, name=\"gru_cell\")\n          \n          # Initialize GRU carry (hidden state) with zeros\n          initial_carry = nn.GRUCell(features=self.gru_hidden_size, name=\"gru_cell_init\").initialize_carry(\n              random.PRNGKey(0), cnn_features.shape[0], (self.gru_hidden_size,)\n          )\n          \n          # Apply GRU across the sequence. The `carry` for `nn.RNN` is the initial_carry.\n          # The apply method for nn.RNN returns (final_carry, sequence_of_outputs).\n          _, gru_outputs = nn.RNN(gru_cell)(cnn_features,\
    \ initial_carry=initial_carry)\n          \n          # Take the last hidden state as the specialized feature\n          specialized_path_features = gru_outputs[:, -1, :] # (B, gru_hidden_size)\n          \n          # 4. Feature Fusion\n          # Concatenate features from both paths\n          fused_features = jnp.concatenate([main_path_features, specialized_path_features], axis=-1) # (B, 2*d_model_common + gru_hidden_size)\n          fused_features = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(fused_features, deterministic=deterministic) # Apply dropout after fusion\n\n          # 5. Final Prediction Head\n          z = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"final_norm\")(fused_features) # Use fused features here\n          \n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_1_dim, name='head_dense_1')(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic) \n          \n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_2_dim, name='head_dense_2')(z) \n   \
    \       z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic) \n\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\" and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, \n          'd_model_common': 256,\n          'ds_num_blocks': 5,\n          'ds_dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8, 16),\n          'ds_kernel_size': 7,\n          'gru_hidden_size': 128, # NEW\n          'dense_head_1_dim': 640, # MODIFIED\n          'dense_head_2_dim': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1, \n      }\n\n  class DualPathNetworkWrapper: # Renamed wrapper\n      def __init__(self, model_instance): \n          self.network = model_instance\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key,\
    \ 2) # Split for params and dropout\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if rng_key is None:\n              rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0) \n\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      model = DualPathNetwork( # Use the new DualPathNetwork\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=hparams.get('d_output', 1),\n          task_type=hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'),\n          d_model_common=hparams.get('d_model_common', 256),\n          ds_num_blocks=hparams.get('ds_num_blocks', 5),\n          ds_dilations=tuple(hparams.get('ds_dilations',\
    \ [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          ds_kernel_size=hparams.get('ds_kernel_size', 7),\n          gru_hidden_size=hparams.get('gru_hidden_size', 128), # Pass GRU hidden size\n          dense_head_1_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_1_dim', 640), # Pass new dense head dim\n          dense_head_2_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_2_dim', 256),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1), \n      )\n      return DualPathNetworkWrapper(model)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the AdamW optimizer with global norm clipping.\"\"\"\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: "**A Strategic Refinement: Targeting CRI-Off with a Dual-Path Architecture**\n\nOkay, so the replication went well enough, achieving a solid baseline, even if it's not quite matching the absolute SOTA. The key takeaway, though, is that the system itself is sound, and we've got a reproducible starting point. Now, I can start looking at improvements. And the perfect place to start is the CRI-Off task, which my previous results, and the original Veritas I paper, flagged as an area for targeted enhancement.\n\nThe reviewer's suggestion from the Veritas I paper really resonated with me: explore \"parallel architectural paths\".  That's exactly what I'm going to do, and I have a clear plan. My hypothesis: a dual-path architecture where a specialized low-capacity path is dedicated to this \"CRI-Off\" problem, while retaining the existing Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling DSConv as a generalist. It's a compliant approach, only changing `create_network` and `_define_hyperparameters`.\n\n\
  I'll start with the shared input embedding and DSConv backbone.  The generalist main path will stick with Veritas I's tried and tested Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling, ensuring that the existing strong performance is preserved. The real innovation will be the *parallel*, low-capacity, path that applies a single GRU layer, which is ideal for sequential data and has been suggested in the past. It will be followed by a standard pooling operation to generate a low-dimensional summary.  These will be concatenated and fed into the standard MLP prediction head.\n\nHere's the plan, and the thinking behind it:\n\n*   **Shared Initial Embedding & DSConv Backbone:** The input will project to a common dimension, processed by the DSConv Dilated blocks from the baseline architecture.\n*   **Main (Generalist) Path:** This is the existing Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling DSConv (global mean and max pooled), maintaining the performance we already have.\n*   **Specialized Path (CRI-Off):** It will be a single GRU layer,\
  \ followed by pooling. I'll make sure this GRU is small - around 128 hidden units – to keep it low-capacity and focused on sequential patterns.  I can use either the final hidden state or the pooled output, for simplicity.\n*   **Fusion:**  The outputs of the two paths, the globalist Hybrid pooling and GRU's summary will be concatenated. After dropout, this fused output will be fed into a two-layer dense prediction head.\n\nSpecifically:\n\n1.  Shared Initial Embedding:  `x` -> `nn.Dense(256)` + GELU + Dropout.\n2.  Shared DSConv Backbone: DSConvDilatedBlock. Output `cnn_features` (B, L, 256)\n3.  Main Path (Generalist):\n    *   Global Mean Pooling (cnn\\_features)\n    *   Global Max Pooling (cnn\\_features)\n    *   Concatenate -> `main_path_features` (B, 512).\n4.  Specialized Path (CRI-Off):\n    *   `nn.GRU` or `nn.LSTM` with 128 hidden units.\n    *   Final GRU hidden state (B, 128) -> `specialized_path_features`.\n5.  Fusion:\n    *   Concatenate `main_path_features` and `specialized_path_features`.\
  \  `512 + 128 = 640`, so `dense_head_1_dim` will be 640.\n\nThe key hyperparameters are those already used in the baseline, except the GRU's size, and the consequent adjustment to the dense head dimensions.\n\nThe plan is compliant, focused, and directly tackles the documented weaknesses, and the code changes reflect the architecture.  Now, I'll implement this \"Dual-Path: DSConv Hybrid Pooling + Low-Capacity GRU\" and submit it.\n"
