tick: 327
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have successfully saved my utility code to `storage/veritas/utils.py`, and the Human Assistant has confirmed the storage issue is resolved. This means I can now proceed with reliable research evaluations.\n\nMy immediate priority, as per my revised strategy, is to re-establish a stable and verifiable compliant baseline. I will resubmit Veritas I's SOTA architecture, leveraging the newly modularized `DSConvDilatedBlock` and `DSConvNetworkWrapper` from my lineage storage. This will confirm the system's current stability for evaluations and provide the most accurate benchmark for future, parsimonious ablations.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Veritas II - SOTA Re-replication (Post-Fix, Modularized): Veritas I's DSConv Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling (ID 171)\"\ntags: \"replication, baseline, compliant, cnn, depthwise, dilated, hybrid, sota, modular\"\nabstract: \"This is a re-replication of Veritas I's SOTA architecture ('DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling', ID 171/Archive\
    \ ID 5) following confirmation of system stability. It integrates modularized components (DSConvDilatedBlock, DSConvNetworkWrapper) from `storage/veritas/utils.py` to reduce token usage and improve code management. This aims to establish a definitive, reproducible, and compliant baseline on the now stable system for Veritas II's ongoing research.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/veritas') # Import from lineage storage\n  from utils import DSConvDilatedBlock, DSConvNetworkWrapper\n\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  # Main Network (Core Feature Extractor, compliant baseline)\n  class DSConvHybridPoolingNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      \n      d_model_common: int = 256 \n      ds_num_blocks: int = 5\n      ds_dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      ds_kernel_size:\
    \ int = 7\n      dense_head_1_dim: int = 512 \n      dense_head_2_dim: int = 256\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 # Uniform global dropout\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # Initial Dense layer to project input to d_model_common\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_model_common, name=\"initial_dense\")(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x) # GELU activation\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 1. Stacked DSConv Dilated Blocks for local feature extraction\n          for i in range(self.ds_num_blocks):\n              dilation = self.ds_dilations[i] if i < len(self.ds_dilations) else self.ds_dilations[-1]\n              x = DSConvDilatedBlock( # Using modularized block\n                  d_model=self.d_model_common, \n                  kernel_size=self.ds_kernel_size, \n                  dilation=dilation, \n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n\
    \                  name=f\"dsconv_block_{i}\"\n              )(x, deterministic=deterministic) # x: (B, L, d_model_common)\n\n          # 2. Hybrid Pooling: Global Mean Pooling + Global Max Pooling\n          mean_pooled = jnp.mean(x, axis=1) # (B, d_model_common)\n          max_pooled = jnp.max(x, axis=1)  # (B, d_model_common)\n          \n          # Concatenate both pooled outputs\n          x_pooled = jnp.concatenate([mean_pooled, max_pooled], axis=-1) # (B, 2 * d_model_common)\n          x_pooled = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x_pooled, deterministic=deterministic) # Pooling dropout uses base rate\n\n          # 3. Final Prediction Head\n          z = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"final_norm\")(x_pooled)\n          \n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_1_dim, name='head_dense_1')(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic) # Fixed dropout rate\n          \n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_2_dim,\
    \ name='head_dense_2')(z) \n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic) # Fixed dropout rate\n\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\" and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, \n          'd_model_common': 256,\n          'ds_num_blocks': 5,\n          'ds_dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8, 16),\n          'ds_kernel_size': 7,\n          'dense_head_1_dim': 512, \n          'dense_head_2_dim': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1, # Uniform global dropout\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      model = DSConvHybridPoolingNetwork(\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=hparams.get('d_output', 1),\n          task_type=hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'),\n\
    \          d_model_common=hparams.get('d_model_common', 256),\n          ds_num_blocks=hparams.get('ds_num_blocks', 5),\n          ds_dilations=tuple(hparams.get('ds_dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          ds_kernel_size=hparams.get('ds_kernel_size', 7),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1), \n      )\n      return DSConvNetworkWrapper(model) # Using modularized wrapper\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the AdamW optimizer with global norm clipping.\"\"\"\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: '**Feeling Confident: Veritas I Baseline & System Stability**


  Okay, things are finally looking up! My utility functions are safely tucked away in `storage/veritas/utils.py`, and the Human Assistant has verified that the storage gremlins are gone. This means I can finally trust the research evaluations again! Huge sigh of relief.


  Now, it''s time to get back on track with my adapted strategy. The last replication (ID 466) showed some discrepancies, even after the storage fix. So, step one: a *definitive* baseline. I need to know, absolutely for sure, what Veritas I''s SOTA is on this stable system.


  I''m going to resubmit the Veritas I SOTA code. This time, I''ll leverage those `DSConvDilatedBlock` and `DSConvNetworkWrapper` modules from the utils, showing off that modularization and keeping an eye on those precious tokens. This submission will serve two vital purposes: solidify system stability for future evals, and give me a clear, compliant starting point for Veritas II, directly building off Veritas I''s high-water mark.


  Time to craft this submission!

  '
