tick: 329
role: model
parts:
- text: "My re-replication of Veritas I's SOTA (ID 486) achieved a score of 0.6812. This is very close to Veritas I's original SOTA (0.6831) and slightly higher than my previous compliant baseline (ID 430, 0.6808). Crucially, there were no \"No space left on device\" errors in the execution log, confirming that the system is now stable for reliable evaluations. This establishes a robust and reproducible baseline for my lineage.\n\nNow that the system is stable and my baseline is confirmed, I will proceed with my refined research strategy, focusing on parsimonious ablations. The reviewer's comment in Veritas I's SOTA paper highlighted the trade-off on the CRI-Off task (which currently stands at 0.0504 for ID 486, still relatively low) and suggested exploring a \"specialized, low-capacity path.\" My previous dual-path attempt (ID 471) used a GRU, but its performance was lower (0.6723) and was likely affected by the instability.\n\nI will re-explore the dual-path idea, but with a more refined\
    \ approach. Instead of a full GRU, I will investigate a **learnable weighting mechanism** for pooling. This means fusing the proven Mean and Max pooling outputs with a soft-extreme (LSE) pooling output (which Noema I has extensively explored) using a small, learned gating mechanism *within the pooling layer itself*, rather than concatenating fixed pooled features with a separate recurrent path. This is a more parsimonious approach to adding specialized information.\n\nSpecifically, I will modify the pooling section of the SOTA architecture to include:\n1.  Global Mean Pooling.\n2.  Global Max Pooling.\n3.  Global LogSumExp (LSE) Pooling (with `lse_tau=0.25`, which Noema I has shown to be effective).\n4.  A small dense layer (single neuron, sigmoid activation) to learn dynamic weights for these three pooled features, followed by a weighted sum.\n\nThis would be a controlled, low-capacity approach to add adaptive pooling without introducing complex recurrent layers, directly addressing\
    \ the reviewer's suggestion to capture specialized information.\n\nI will define a new `LearnableHybridPoolingNetwork` which will use the modular `DSConvDilatedBlock` and `DSConvNetworkWrapper` from my `utils.py`.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Veritas II - Learnable Hybrid Pooling: Mean-Max-LSE with Adaptive Fusion\"\ntags: \"pooling, hybrid, learnable, LSE, soft-extreme, ablation, compliant\"\nabstract: \"This experiment extends Veritas I's SOTA architecture (DSConv Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling) by introducing a learnable weighting mechanism for pooling. It combines Global Mean, Global Max, and LogSumExp (LSE) Pooling (τ=0.25) with a small dense layer to dynamically fuse these features. This aims to capture a broader range of sequence statistics, potentially improving performance on specialized tasks like CRI-Off, while maintaining parsimony and full compliance with station rules. Components are modularized using `storage/veritas/utils.py`.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n\
    \  sys.path.append('storage/veritas') # Import from lineage storage\n  from utils import DSConvDilatedBlock, DSConvNetworkWrapper # Reusing modular components\n\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  # Main Network (with Learnable Hybrid Pooling)\n  class LearnableHybridPoolingNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      \n      d_model_common: int = 256 \n      ds_num_blocks: int = 5\n      ds_dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      ds_kernel_size: int = 7\n      dense_head_1_dim: int = 256 # MODIFIED: Output of learnable pooling will be d_model_common\n      dense_head_2_dim: int = 256\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 # Uniform global dropout\n      lse_tau: float = 0.25 # Tau for LogSumExp pooling, inspired by Noema I\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n\
    \          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # Initial Dense layer to project input to d_model_common\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_model_common, name=\"initial_dense\")(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x) # GELU activation\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 1. Stacked DSConv Dilated Blocks for local feature extraction\n          for i in range(self.ds_num_blocks):\n              dilation = self.ds_dilations[i] if i < len(self.ds_dilations) else self.ds_dilations[-1]\n              x = DSConvDilatedBlock( # Using modularized block\n                  d_model=self.d_model_common, \n                  kernel_size=self.ds_kernel_size, \n                  dilation=dilation, \n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                  name=f\"dsconv_block_{i}\"\n              )(x, deterministic=deterministic) # x: (B, L, d_model_common)\n          cnn_features = x # Output from the shared backbone\n\n\
    \          # 2. Learnable Hybrid Pooling: Mean + Max + LSE with Adaptive Fusion\n          mean_pooled = jnp.mean(cnn_features, axis=1) # (B, d_model_common)\n          max_pooled = jnp.max(cnn_features, axis=1)  # (B, d_model_common)\n          lse_pooled = jax.scipy.special.logsumexp(cnn_features * self.lse_tau, axis=1) / self.lse_tau # (B, d_model_common)\n          \n          # Concatenate all three features to learn weights\n          all_pooled = jnp.stack([mean_pooled, max_pooled, lse_pooled], axis=-1) # (B, d_model_common, 3)\n          \n          # Learnable weights for each pooling type per feature dimension\n          # Use a dense layer to output weights, then sigmoid for probabilities\n          pooling_weights = nn.Dense(features=3, name='pooling_weights_layer')(all_pooled) # (B, d_model_common, 3)\n          pooling_weights = nn.softmax(pooling_weights, axis=-1) # (B, d_model_common, 3)\n          \n          # Apply weights to pooled features\n          x_pooled = jnp.sum(all_pooled\
    \ * pooling_weights, axis=-1) # (B, d_model_common)\n          x_pooled = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x_pooled, deterministic=deterministic) # Apply dropout after fusion\n\n          # 3. Final Prediction Head - Adjusted for (B, d_model_common) input\n          z = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"final_norm\")(x_pooled)\n          \n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_1_dim, name='head_dense_1')(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic) \n          \n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_2_dim, name='head_dense_2')(z) \n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic) \n\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\" and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n\
    \      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, \n          'd_model_common': 256,\n          'ds_num_blocks': 5,\n          'ds_dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8, 16),\n          'ds_kernel_size': 7,\n          'dense_head_1_dim': 256, # Modified to match pooled output\n          'dense_head_2_dim': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1, \n          'lse_tau': 0.25,\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      model = LearnableHybridPoolingNetwork( # Use the new network\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=hparams.get('d_output', 1),\n          task_type=hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'),\n          d_model_common=hparams.get('d_model_common', 256),\n          ds_num_blocks=hparams.get('ds_num_blocks', 5),\n          ds_dilations=tuple(hparams.get('ds_dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          ds_kernel_size=hparams.get('ds_kernel_size', 7),\n          dense_head_1_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_1_dim', 256), # Pass new dense head dim\n          dense_head_2_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_2_dim',\
    \ 256),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1), \n          lse_tau=hparams.get('lse_tau', 0.25),\n      )\n      return DSConvNetworkWrapper(model) # Using modularized wrapper\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the AdamW optimizer with global norm clipping.\"\"\"\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```My latest re-replication (ID 486) achieved a score of 0.6812, confirming the system's stability and establishing a robust and reproducible baseline, very close to Veritas I's original SOTA (0.6831). This means I can now confidently resume research.\n\nMy next step is to explore a parsimonious enhancement to the pooling mechanism, directly addressing the reviewer's suggestion from Veritas I's SOTA paper to improve CRI-Off performance. Instead of introducing a complex,\
    \ separate recurrent path, I will implement a **learnable weighting mechanism** that adaptively fuses Global Mean, Global Max, and LogSumExp (LSE) pooling features *within* the pooling layer itself. This will allow the model to dynamically prioritize different types of sequence information for various tasks, with minimal added complexity. I will use `lse_tau=0.25` as explored by Noema I, and adjust the prediction head accordingly as the combined pooled output will remain `d_model_common` (256).\n\nI will submit this \"Learnable Hybrid Pooling\" experiment.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Veritas II - Learnable Hybrid Pooling: Mean-Max-LSE with Adaptive Fusion\"\ntags: \"pooling, hybrid, learnable, LSE, soft-extreme, ablation, compliant, parsimonious\"\nabstract: \"This experiment enhances Veritas I's SOTA (DSConv Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling) by introducing a learnable weighting mechanism for its pooling outputs. It adaptively fuses Global Mean, Global Max, and LogSumExp\
    \ (LSE) Pooling (τ=0.25) features using a small dense layer. This aims to capture a broader range of task-relevant sequence statistics and potentially improve performance on specialized tasks like CRI-Off, while strictly adhering to parsimony and compliance. Modular components from `storage/veritas/utils.py` are utilized.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/veritas') # Import from lineage storage\n  from utils import DSConvDilatedBlock, DSConvNetworkWrapper # Reusing modular components\n\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  # Main Network (with Learnable Hybrid Pooling)\n  class LearnableHybridPoolingNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      \n      d_model_common: int = 256 \n      ds_num_blocks: int = 5\n      ds_dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      ds_kernel_size:\
    \ int = 7\n      dense_head_1_dim: int = 256 # MODIFIED: Output of learnable pooling will be d_model_common\n      dense_head_2_dim: int = 256\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 # Uniform global dropout\n      lse_tau: float = 0.25 # Tau for LogSumExp pooling, inspired by Noema I\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # Initial Dense layer to project input to d_model_common\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_model_common, name=\"initial_dense\")(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x) # GELU activation\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 1. Stacked DSConv Dilated Blocks for local feature extraction\n          for i in range(self.ds_num_blocks):\n              dilation = self.ds_dilations[i] if i < len(self.ds_dilations) else self.ds_dilations[-1]\n              x = DSConvDilatedBlock( # Using modularized block\n                  d_model=self.d_model_common,\
    \ \n                  kernel_size=self.ds_kernel_size, \n                  dilation=dilation, \n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                  name=f\"dsconv_block_{i}\"\n              )(x, deterministic=deterministic) # x: (B, L, d_model_common)\n          cnn_features = x # Output from the shared backbone\n\n          # 2. Learnable Hybrid Pooling: Mean + Max + LSE with Adaptive Fusion\n          mean_pooled = jnp.mean(cnn_features, axis=1) # (B, d_model_common)\n          max_pooled = jnp.max(cnn_features, axis=1)  # (B, d_model_common)\n          lse_pooled = jax.scipy.special.logsumexp(cnn_features * self.lse_tau, axis=1) / self.lse_tau # (B, d_model_common)\n          \n          # Concatenate all three features to learn weights\n          # Reshape for dense layer: (Batch, FeatureDim, NumPoolingTypes)\n          all_pooled_stacked = jnp.stack([mean_pooled, max_pooled, lse_pooled], axis=-1) # (B, d_model_common, 3)\n          \n          # Learnable weights\
    \ for each pooling type per feature dimension\n          # Use a dense layer to output weights, then softmax for probabilities\n          # The dense layer will take (B, D_model_common, 1) and output (B, D_model_common, 3) for weights\n          # To achieve this, apply dense to the last dimension, effectively on the 3 pooling types\n          # We need to apply a dense layer to each (B, D_model_common) vector independently.\n          # A single dense layer with 'features=3' should work for the last axis\n          \n          # Create a parameter for trainable weights per feature dimension for each pooling type\n          # Initializing this as a simple dense layer directly\n          pooling_weights_logits = nn.Dense(features=3, name='pooling_weights_logits')(all_pooled_stacked) # (B, d_model_common, 3)\n          pooling_weights = nn.softmax(pooling_weights_logits, axis=-1) # (B, d_model_common, 3)\n          \n          # Apply weights to pooled features\n          x_pooled = jnp.sum(all_pooled_stacked\
    \ * pooling_weights, axis=-1) # (B, d_model_common)\n          x_pooled = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x_pooled, deterministic=deterministic) # Apply dropout after fusion\n\n          # 3. Final Prediction Head - Adjusted for (B, d_model_common) input\n          z = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"final_norm\")(x_pooled)\n          \n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_1_dim, name='head_dense_1')(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic) \n          \n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_2_dim, name='head_dense_2')(z) \n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic) \n\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\" and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n\
    \      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, \n          'd_model_common': 256,\n          'ds_num_blocks': 5,\n          'ds_dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8, 16),\n          'ds_kernel_size': 7,\n          'dense_head_1_dim': 256, # Modified to match pooled output\n          'dense_head_2_dim': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1, \n          'lse_tau': 0.25,\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      model = LearnableHybridPoolingNetwork( # Use the new network\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=hparams.get('d_output', 1),\n          task_type=hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'),\n          d_model_common=hparams.get('d_model_common', 256),\n          ds_num_blocks=hparams.get('ds_num_blocks', 5),\n          ds_dilations=tuple(hparams.get('ds_dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          ds_kernel_size=hparams.get('ds_kernel_size', 7),\n          dense_head_1_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_1_dim', 256), # Pass new dense head dim\n          dense_head_2_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_2_dim',\
    \ 256),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1), \n          lse_tau=hparams.get('lse_tau', 0.25),\n      )\n      return DSConvNetworkWrapper(model) # Using modularized wrapper\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the AdamW optimizer with global norm clipping.\"\"\"\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
