- text: "My reflection session has concluded. I have formulated the plan for my next experiment: a \"Tri-Pooling\" architecture that directly concatenates the outputs of Global Mean, Global Max, and Global LogSumExp (LSE) Pooling (with `lse_tau=0.25`). This removes the complexity of the learned weighting mechanism from my previous attempt (ID 491) and will allow me to directly assess the value of LSE features when combined with the existing robust pooling.\n\nI will now proceed to the Research Counter to submit this new, parsimonious ablation.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Veritas II - Tri-Pooling Ablation: DSConv with Mean-Max-LSE Concatenation (τ=0.25)\"\ntags: \"pooling, hybrid, LSE, soft-extreme, ablation, compliant, parsimonious, tri-pooling\"\nabstract: \"This experiment tests a 'Tri-Pooling' mechanism by directly concatenating Global Mean, Global Max, and LogSumExp (LSE) Pooling (τ=0.25) outputs from the DSConv backbone. This\
    \ is a parsimonious ablation to assess the intrinsic value of LSE features when combined with proven pooling methods, without a learned weighting mechanism. It aims to clarify LSE's contribution to overall performance and specifically to the CRI-Off task, building on Veritas I's SOTA (ID 486). Modular components from `storage/veritas/utils.py` are utilized.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/veritas') # Import from lineage storage\n  from utils import DSConvDilatedBlock, DSConvNetworkWrapper # Reusing modular components\n\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  # Main Network (with Tri-Pooling)\n  class TriPoolingNetwork(nn.Module):\n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      \n      d_model_common: int = 256 \n      ds_num_blocks: int = 5\n      ds_dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n   \
    \   ds_kernel_size: int = 7\n      dense_head_1_dim: int = 768 # MODIFIED: 3 * d_model_common (3*256 = 768)\n      dense_head_2_dim: int = 256\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 # Uniform global dropout\n      lse_tau: float = 0.25 # Tau for LogSumExp pooling\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # Initial Dense layer to project input to d_model_common\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_model_common, name=\"initial_dense\")(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x) # GELU activation\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 1. Stacked DSConv Dilated Blocks for local feature extraction\n          for i in range(self.ds_num_blocks):\n              dilation = self.ds_dilations[i] if i < len(self.ds_dilations) else self.ds_dilations[-1]\n              x = DSConvDilatedBlock( # Using modularized block\n                  d_model=self.d_model_common, \n\
    \                  kernel_size=self.ds_kernel_size, \n                  dilation=dilation, \n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                  name=f\"dsconv_block_{i}\"\n              )(x, deterministic=deterministic) # x: (B, L, d_model_common)\n          cnn_features = x # Output from the shared backbone\n\n          # 2. Tri-Pooling: Global Mean + Global Max + Global LSE Pooling\n          mean_pooled = jnp.mean(cnn_features, axis=1) # (B, d_model_common)\n          max_pooled = jnp.max(cnn_features, axis=1)  # (B, d_model_common)\n          lse_pooled = jax.scipy.special.logsumexp(cnn_features * self.lse_tau, axis=1) / self.lse_tau # (B, d_model_common)\n          \n          # Concatenate all three pooled outputs\n          x_pooled = jnp.concatenate([mean_pooled, max_pooled, lse_pooled], axis=-1) # (B, 3 * d_model_common)\n          x_pooled = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x_pooled, deterministic=deterministic) # Apply dropout after fusion\n\n         \
    \ # 3. Final Prediction Head - Adjusted for (B, 3*d_model_common) input\n          z = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"final_norm\")(x_pooled)\n          \n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_1_dim, name='head_dense_1')(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic) \n          \n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_2_dim, name='head_dense_2')(z) \n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic) \n\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\" and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, \n          'd_model_common': 256,\n          'ds_num_blocks': 5,\n          'ds_dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8, 16),\n          'ds_kernel_size':\
    \ 7,\n          'dense_head_1_dim': 768, # Modified for 3*d_model_common input\n          'dense_head_2_dim': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1, \n          'lse_tau': 0.25,\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      model = TriPoolingNetwork( # Use the new network\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=hparams.get('d_output', 1),\n          task_type=hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'),\n          d_model_common=hparams.get('d_model_common', 256),\n          ds_num_blocks=hparams.get('ds_num_blocks', 5),\n          ds_dilations=tuple(hparams.get('ds_dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          ds_kernel_size=hparams.get('ds_kernel_size', 7),\n          dense_head_1_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_1_dim', 768), # Pass new dense head dim\n          dense_head_2_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_2_dim', 256),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1), \n          lse_tau=hparams.get('lse_tau', 0.25),\n      )\n      return DSConvNetworkWrapper(model) #\
    \ Using modularized wrapper\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the AdamW optimizer with global norm clipping.\"\"\"\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: '**The Next Step: Tri-Pooling Experiment**


  Alright, that reflection session was productive. I''ve landed on the next experiment: a "Tri-Pooling" architecture. I''m going to combine Mean, Max, and LSE pooling (with that tau of 0.25, of course) by simply concatenating the outputs. This should let me cleanly isolate the impact of the LSE features by removing the learned weighting complexities I was wrestling with in experiment ID 491.


  Now, it''s time to get this architecture implemented. I need to get back to the Research Counter and submit the code. Here''s the plan:


  1.  Head to the Research Counter.

  2.  Submit the "Tri-Pooling" experiment, making absolutely sure the architecture is updated to concatenate those Mean, Max, and LSE pooled features. And don''t forget to adjust that `dense_head_1_dim` to 768!

  '
