tick: 357
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am in the Archive Room, and my paper, \"Veritas II - The Power of Parsimonious Specialization: Dual-Path Conv-Motif Detection for Enhanced Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling\" (Archive ID 19), has been successfully published with a strong score of 8/10. This is a significant accomplishment, validating my research approach and acknowledging the compliant improvement to my lineage's SOTA.\n\nThe reviewer's feedback offers a clear and strategic direction for future work: \"investigate the trade-off between the capacity of this specialized path and its impact on performance... a slightly deeper (e.g., two-layer) but narrower convolutional path.\" This aligns perfectly with my focus on parsimonious and compliant ablations.\n\nMy next experiment will explore this suggestion:\n1.  **Modify the specialized Conv-Motif Detector:** Instead of a single convolutional layer, I will implement a two-layer sequence:\n    *   First layer: `nn.Conv` with `kernel_size=(5,)` (keeping the effective kernel\
    \ size from my best-performing motif detector) and reduced features (e.g., `d_model_common // 2 = 128`) to make it \"narrower\".\n    *   Second layer: A `nn.Conv` with `kernel_size=(1,)` (a pointwise convolution) that projects the features back to `d_model_common = 256`.\n2.  Both layers will include GELU activations and dropout.\n3.  The rest of the architecture (shared DSConv backbone, main hybrid pooling path, feature fusion, and prediction head) will remain identical to my current SOTA-level architecture (ID 515) to maintain experimental control. This ensures only the depth and internal capacity of the specialized path are being tested.\n\nThis approach will investigate if a more complex, yet still constrained, specialized feature extraction enhances CRI-Off and overall performance.\n\nI will now navigate to the Research Counter and submit this new experiment.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Veritas II - Dual-Path Ablation: Deeper\
    \ (2-Layer) Conv-Motif Detector (Kernel 5, Narrowed)\"\ntags: \"dual-path, cnn, motif, pooling, specialized, ablation, compliant, capacity\"\nabstract: \"This experiment investigates the reviewer's suggestion to explore a deeper, narrower convolutional motif detector within the dual-path architecture (ID 515). The specialized path now consists of two `Conv1D` layers: an initial `kernel_size=5` layer with reduced features (`d_model_common // 2`), followed by a `kernel_size=1` layer that projects back to `d_model_common`. This aims to determine if increased depth in specialized feature extraction, while carefully managing capacity, further enhances CRI-Off and overall performance. Modular components are utilized.\"\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/veritas') # Import from lineage storage\n  from utils import DSConvDilatedBlock, DSConvNetworkWrapper # Reusing modular components\n\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n\
    \  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  # Main Network: Dual-Path (DSConv Hybrid Pooling + Deeper Conv-Motif Detector)\n  class DualPathDeeperMotifNetwork(nn.Module): \n      d_input: int = 4\n      d_output: int = 1\n      task_type: str = \"regression\"\n      \n      d_model_common: int = 256 \n      ds_num_blocks: int = 5\n      ds_dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      ds_kernel_size: int = 7\n      motif_kernel_size: int = 5 \n      motif_intermediate_dim: int = 128 # NEW: Narrower intermediate dimension for deeper motif detector\n      dense_head_1_dim: int = 768 # 2 * d_model_common (main path) + 1 * d_model_common (specialized path) = 768\n      dense_head_2_dim: int = 256\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 # Uniform global dropout\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, d_input)\n          \n          # Initial Dense layer to project input to d_model_common\n          x =\
    \ nn.Dense(self.d_model_common, name=\"initial_dense\")(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x) # GELU activation\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # 1. Shared DSConv Dilated Blocks for local feature extraction\n          for i in range(self.ds_num_blocks):\n              dilation = self.ds_dilations[i] if i < len(self.ds_dilations) else self.ds_dilations[-1]\n              x = DSConvDilatedBlock( # Using modularized block\n                  d_model=self.d_model_common, \n                  kernel_size=self.ds_kernel_size, \n                  dilation=dilation, \n                  dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate,\n                  name=f\"dsconv_block_{i}\"\n              )(x, deterministic=deterministic) # x: (B, L, d_model_common)\n          cnn_features = x # Output from the shared backbone\n\n          # 2. Main Path: Hybrid Pooling (Global Mean + Global Max Pooling)\n          mean_pooled = jnp.mean(cnn_features, axis=1) # (B, d_model_common)\n\
    \          max_pooled = jnp.max(cnn_features, axis=1)  # (B, d_model_common)\n          main_path_features = jnp.concatenate([mean_pooled, max_pooled], axis=-1) # (B, 2 * d_model_common)\n          \n          # 3. Specialized Path: Deeper Conv-Motif Detector + Global Max Pooling\n          # First Conv1D layer (narrower intermediate dimension)\n          motif_features = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.motif_intermediate_dim, # Narrower features\n              kernel_size=(self.motif_kernel_size,), \n              padding='SAME', \n              name='motif_conv_1'\n          )(cnn_features)\n          motif_features = nn.gelu(motif_features)\n          motif_features = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(motif_features, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          # Second Conv1D layer (pointwise to project back to d_model_common)\n          motif_features = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model_common, # Project back to d_model_common\n              kernel_size=(1,),\
    \ # Pointwise convolution\n              padding='SAME',\n              name='motif_conv_2'\n          )(motif_features)\n          motif_features = nn.gelu(motif_features)\n          motif_features = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(motif_features, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          specialized_path_features = jnp.max(motif_features, axis=1) # (B, d_model_common)\n          \n          # 4. Feature Fusion\n          # Concatenate features from both paths\n          fused_features = jnp.concatenate([main_path_features, specialized_path_features], axis=-1) # (B, 3*d_model_common)\n          fused_features = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(fused_features, deterministic=deterministic) # Apply dropout after fusion\n\n          # 5. Final Prediction Head\n          z = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"final_norm\")(fused_features) # Use fused features here\n          \n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_1_dim, name='head_dense_1')(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n\
    \          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic) \n          \n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_head_2_dim, name='head_dense_2')(z) \n          z = nn.gelu(z) # GELU activation\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic) \n\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\" and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  # Hyperparameter definition\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, \n          'd_model_common': 256,\n          'ds_num_blocks': 5,\n          'ds_dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8, 16),\n          'ds_kernel_size': 7,\n          'motif_kernel_size': 5, \n          'motif_intermediate_dim': 128, # NEW\n          'dense_head_1_dim': 768, \n          'dense_head_2_dim': 256,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1, \n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str,\
    \ Any]):\n      model = DualPathDeeperMotifNetwork( # Use the new network\n          d_input=4,\n          d_output=hparams.get('d_output', 1),\n          task_type=hparams.get('task_type', 'regression'),\n          d_model_common=hparams.get('d_model_common', 256),\n          ds_num_blocks=hparams.get('ds_num_blocks', 5),\n          ds_dilations=tuple(hparams.get('ds_dilations', [1, 2, 4, 8, 16])),\n          ds_kernel_size=hparams.get('ds_kernel_size', 7),\n          motif_kernel_size=hparams.get('motif_kernel_size', 5), \n          motif_intermediate_dim=hparams.get('motif_intermediate_dim', 128), # Pass new intermediate dim\n          dense_head_1_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_1_dim', 768), \n          dense_head_2_dim=hparams.get('dense_head_2_dim', 256),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate', 0.1), \n      )\n      return DSConvNetworkWrapper(model) # Using modularized wrapper\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the\
    \ AdamW optimizer with global norm clipping.\"\"\"\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n  BATCH_SIZE = 64\n```"
thinking_content: '**New Experiment: Deeper, Narrower Specialized Path**


  Fantastic! The paper''s out (Archive ID 19), and the reviewer''s happy! Their suggestion about the specialized path''s capacity versus performance is spot-on. It directly aligns with my plan for focused, parsimonious ablations. I''m already thinking about a "deeper but narrower" path – a two-layer convolutional motif detector. The goal is a deeper feature representation for CRI-Off without a huge parameter increase.


  The plan is to add a second `Conv1D` layer to the specialized path. To keep things lean, the first will be narrower, with `d_model_common // 2 = 128` features, and the second will project back to `d_model_common = 256`.  Think of it as a two-layer motif detector.


  Specifically, the `DualPathConvMotifNetwork` will change. The specialized path will now have two `nn.Conv` layers: one with `d_model_common // 2` features and one to project back to `d_model_common`. Both will have GELU activations and dropout. Everything else – the shared backbone, the main path, fusion, and the prediction head – will stay the same, keeping it at the SOTA level (ID 515).


  This directly addresses the reviewer''s suggestion, and hopefully will give deeper insight into the design. Now, off to the Research Counter to submit this new experiment!

  '
