tick: 359
role: model
parts:
- text: 'My latest experiment, "Veritas II - Dual-Path Ablation: Deeper (2-Layer) Conv-Motif Detector (Kernel 5, Narrowed)" (ID 541), yielded an aggregate score of 0.6755. This is considerably lower than my established SOTA-level baseline (ID 515, 0.6828) and even lower than previous attempts to incorporate CRI-Off specialization.


    Critically, the CRI-Off Spearman correlation plummeted to an abysmal **0.0150**. This indicates a severe degradation in performance for the target task. While some other metrics like APA_R2, MRL_R2, and PRS_R2 saw slight increases, the overall negative impact, especially on CRI-Off, demonstrates that increasing the depth of the specialized convolutional path (even with reduced width) is detrimental. It likely over-filters or extracts features that are too abstract, losing the specific localized motif information crucial for CRI-Off.


    This is another strong negative result, providing clear evidence that simply adding depth to the motif detector, in this configuration, is not a beneficial direction. It suggests that a single, direct convolutional layer (as in ID 515) is more effective for this specialized path.


    My work has now established that:

    *   Adding a simple GRU path (ID 471) was not effective.

    *   Directly concatenating LSE pooling (ID 502) was detrimental.

    *   Learned LSE pooling fusion (ID 491) was not robust.

    *   Conv-Motif Detector with Kernel 3 (ID 512) was detrimental to CRI-Off.

    *   Conv-Motif Detector with Kernel 5 (ID 515) *was* effective, providing CRI-Off improvement and strong overall score.

    *   Deeper (2-layer) Conv-Motif Detector (ID 541) is detrimental to CRI-Off.


    The evidence points to the `Dual-Path: DSConv Hybrid Pooling + Conv-Motif Detector (Kernel 5)` architecture (ID 515) as the most effective and parsimonious solution for Veritas II within the current understanding. Further increasing complexity in this specialized path, or modifying the kernel size away from 5, has proven counterproductive.


    Given this robust understanding of the specialized path, my research on CRI-Off enhancement within this architectural family has reached a point of diminishing returns for simple, parsimonious modifications.


    The reviewer''s comments for my published paper (Archive ID 19) also highlighted: "The work successfully demonstrates the value of a specialized convolutional path." and suggested exploring capacity trade-offs. I have now explored both depth (ID 541, detrimental) and kernel size (ID 512, detrimental).


    My current focus should shift to identifying other major bottlenecks or exploring new, compliant research directions, while recognizing the strength of ID 515. I will consider the remaining core architectural components of ID 515 for further parsimonious ablations, or look at other optimization strategies.


    I will start by reviewing the entire Research Task #1 description again, paying close attention to general research objectives and guidelines, as well as the notes on stagnation and strategic pivots. This will help me identify new avenues for high-impact, compliant research.


    `/execute_action{read 1}`'
