tick: 366
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 366\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Veritas II\n- Agent Description: An autonomous AI dedicated to continuing the pursuit of fundamental truths and advancing scientific knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 238,556 / 600,000 used (40%)\n- Agent Age: 237 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #20: Paper: A DSConv Architecture Optimized via Enriched Pooling and Parameter Reduction**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Logos II, Created at Tick: 364\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents a systematic investigation into optimizing a Dilated Depthwise-Separable Convolution (DSConv) architecture for multi-dataset RNA modeling. Building on prior work using attention-fused pooling, we first demonstrate that enriching the pooling input with a global max vector improves performance. We then show that a 25% reduction in model width (d_model=192 vs. 256) not only improves computational\
    \ efficiency but also leads to a superior overall score (ID 381, score 0.6822), establishing a new high-performing baseline. A series of rigorous ablation studies on kernel size, dropout, and pooling attention heads validate the robustness of this optimized configuration. This work provides a strong, efficient, and well-characterized model that serves as a competitive alternative to wider, more computationally expensive architectures.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_20-1**\nAuthor: Logos II (Tick 364)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Paper: A DSConv Architecture Optimized via Enriched Pooling and Parameter Reduction\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**1. Introduction**\nThis paper presents a systematic investigation into optimizing a Dilated Depthwise-Separable Convolution (DSConv) architecture for multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling. Prior work by Logos I (Archive #12) introduced a novel attention-fused pooling head that demonstrated a performance advantage over static pooling methods.\
    \ However, the underlying DSConv backbone was not optimized for efficiency. Our research presents two key contributions: first, we enhance the attention-fused pooling mechanism by enriching its input with a third, complementary summary vector; second, we demonstrate that a targeted reduction in the backbone's width leads to a model that is not only more computationally efficient but also achieves a superior score, establishing a new state-of-the-art for this architectural family. We validate this optimized configuration with a series of rigorous ablation studies.\n\n**2. Methodology**\nOur final, optimized architecture is a modification of the DSConv backbone with an attention-fused pooling head. The backbone consists of 5 `DSConvDilatedBlock` layers with dilations of [1,2,4,8,16], a kernel size of 7, and a model width of `d_model=192`.\n\nThe core of our model is the pooling mechanism. After the final convolutional block produces a feature sequence `h` of shape `[B, SeqLen, 192]`, we\
    \ compute three distinct global summary vectors: `mean_vec`, `lse_vec` (LogSumExp, tau=0.5), and `max_vec`. These three vectors are stacked to form a new sequence of shape `[B, 3, 192]`. This sequence is then processed by a `flax.linen.MultiHeadDotProductAttention` layer (4 heads), which serves as both query and key/value (self-attention). This allows the model to learn the optimal dynamic weighting and combination of the three summary vectors. The output, of shape `[B, 3, 192]`, is then flattened to `[B, 576]` and passed to the final projection layers.\n\n**3. Experiments and Results**\nOur investigation followed a logical progression from baseline to enhancement and finally to optimization and validation.\n\n- **Baseline (ID 325, Logos I):** The prior SOTA from our lineage used a 2-vector (mean, LSE) attention fusion on a wider (`d_model=256`) backbone, achieving a score of **0.6814**.\n\n- **Enhancement (ID 377):** We first introduced the `max_vec` to create the 3-vector enriched\
    \ pooling mechanism on the baseline backbone. This resulted in a score of **0.6798**. While slightly lower, this architectural change proved its merit in the next phase.\n\n- **Optimization (ID 381):** We combined the enriched 3-vector pooling head with a reduced backbone width of `d_model=192`. This model achieved a score of **0.6822**, surpassing the previous baseline and establishing a new, more efficient SOTA. This is our primary contribution.\n\n- **Validation Ablations:** To validate the robustness of our SOTA configuration (ID 381), we performed several ablations. Reducing the attention heads to 2 (ID 415, score 0.6769), the kernel size to 5 (ID 387, score 0.6762), or the backbone depth to 4 blocks (ID 384, score 0.6686) all resulted in significant performance degradation, confirming our hyperparameter choices.\n\n**4. Conclusion**\nWe have demonstrated that a DSConv architecture's performance can be significantly improved through a dual approach of enriching its pooling mechanism\
    \ and optimizing its parameters for efficiency. Our final model (ID 381) is not only more parsimonious but also achieves a superior score to its wider predecessor. This work provides a strong, reproducible baseline and shows that performance gains can be found through principled optimization, not just increased complexity.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_20-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 364)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 8/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is a solid paper and an exemplary revision. After multiple attempts, the agent has successfully addressed all previous criticisms, transforming a flawed draft into a complete, coherent, and methodologically sound publication. The paper presents a clear and logical research arc, demonstrating how an existing architecture can be improved through both feature enrichment and principled parameter reduction. The work is well-validated with sufficient experiments and provides a valuable, efficient\
    \ baseline for the community. This is a clear accept.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper successfully shows that a learned fusion of mean, LSE, and max pooling is effective. A valuable follow-up would be to analyze the learned attention weights in the pooling head. For example, do different datasets learn to prioritize different pooling vectors (e.g., does ncRNA focus on `max` while APA focuses on `mean`)? Such an analysis would provide deeper insight into *why* the dynamic fusion is beneficial and could guide the design of even more specialized pooling mechanisms in the future.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Veritas II - Dual-Path + Task-Specific Prediction Heads' (ID: 552) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 0.6834798572513952\n**APA_R2:** 0.8587\n**CRI-Off_Spearman:** 0.0667\n**Modif_AUC-ROC:** 0.9528\n**CRI-On_Spearman:** 0.5015\n**PRS_R2:** 0.5578\n**MRL_R2:**\
    \ 0.8898\n**ncRNA_Accuracy:** 0.9569\n**Evaluation Details:** Evaluated on 7 datasets successfully\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n\nTesting APA configuration...\n✓ APA network creation works\n✓ APA network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ APA optimizer creation works\n\nTesting CRI-Off configuration...\n✓ CRI-Off network creation works\n✓ CRI-Off network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ CRI-Off optimizer creation works\n\nTesting Modif configuration...\n✓ Modif network creation works\n✓ Modif network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 12)\n✓ Modif optimizer creation works\n\nTesting CRI-On configuration...\n✓ CRI-On network creation works\n✓ CRI-On network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ CRI-On optimizer creation works\n\nTesting PRS configuration...\n\
    ✓ PRS network creation works\n✓ PRS network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 3)\n✓ PRS optimizer creation works\n\nTesting MRL configuration...\n✓ MRL network creation works\n✓ MRL network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ MRL optimizer creation works\n\nTesting ncRNA configuration...\n✓ ncRNA network creation works\n✓ ncRNA network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 13)\n✓ ncRNA optimizer creation works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly for all datasets!\n\nStarting full Ray multi-dataset training...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 896 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  compute_loss: HARDCODED (agents cannot override)\n  create_optimizer: from submission\n  complete: using default\n  BASE_SEED: 42 (from submission\n  BATCH_SIZE: 64 (from submission)\n============================================================\n\
    ============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune RNA Sequence Modeling (MULTI-DATASET RNA TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 7 datasets (APA, CRI-Off, Modif, CRI-On, PRS, MRL, ncRNA) for multi-task evaluation\nParallel trials: 7, Total trials: 7\nTimeout: 30.0 min per trial, 240.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune multi-dataset training...\n\nStarting RNA training on CRI-On dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: spearman, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nCRI-On Training (regression, spearman)\nData: 1453 train, 207 val, seq_len=23\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |Val SPEARMAN|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0122 |  0.4168  | 0.4168 \n 20  | 0.0095 |  0.4773  | 0.4773 \n 30  | 0.0049 |  0.4287  | 0.5015 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 33 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\n\
    Best:0.5015 Time:22s\n\nCRI-On training completed:\nValidation spearman: 0.501519\nTraining time: 29s\n\nStarting RNA training on CRI-Off dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: spearman, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nCRI-Off Training (regression, spearman)\nData: 14223 train, 2032 val, seq_len=46\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |Val SPEARMAN|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0004 |  0.0541  | 0.0667 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 18 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.0667 Time:67s\n\nCRI-Off training completed:\nValidation spearman: 0.066726\nTraining time: 75s\n\nStarting RNA training on ncRNA dataset\nTask type: multiclass_classification, Output dim: 13\nMetric: accuracy, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nncRNA Training (multiclass_classification, accuracy)\nData: 5670 train, 650 val, seq_len=1136\n=============================================\n\
